WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.434
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.210
PROFESSOR: Yeah, welcome
all to the ninth lecture

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.970
series in our fall series,
God and Computers--

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.840
Minds, Machines,
and Metaphysics.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.840
I welcome you all, and
specifically the students

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.506
who are at the end
of the term and have,

00:00:23.506 --> 00:00:25.440
certainly, a lot of
other things to do.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.760
Welcome here.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.190
For all people who
want to discuss

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.880
a little bit beyond the question
sessions we have tonight--

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.950
and usually after
the question series--

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.190
on Monday, December 7th,
at Harvard Divinity School

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.030
at 12 o'clock, Harvey Cox and me
run, again, a discussion group

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.580
on this lecture series.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.770
12 o'clock, Harvard
Divinity School on Monday.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.350
So for all people who want
to talk about issues which

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.960
go a little bit
beyond what we can

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.250
answer in this lecture series.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.440
So far, we had two
different paragraphs

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.910
in this lecture series.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:03.090
The first four lectures
in the series dealt with,

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.650
what does it mean to be human?

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.350
What is the so-called
human factor?

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.420
The second lecture-- the second
part of the lecture series,

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.830
then the next four lectures
presented case studies

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:18.510
from a Catholic, a Jewish, a
Hindu, and Buddhist perspective

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.450
on how scientists deal
with existential issues

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.880
within and outside
of their research.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.940
And the last two
lectures, we have today

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.210
and next Wednesday are
lectures dealing now

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.250
going back to computer
science, and deal

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.880
with questions on computer
science, computers, and God.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.230
And so I'm particularly happy
to welcome, today, Ray Kurzweil.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:44.570
Ray Kurzweil is actually founder
of no less than four companies.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.250
One is Kurzweil
Technologies Incorporation,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.220
which is a consulting
firm, patent firms,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.170
and other consulting issues.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:55.740
The other one is Kurzweil
Education System.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.130
Ray Kurzweil has invented
a couple of very innovative

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.240
technologies here.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.360
In that company, he
distributes reading software

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.920
for blind people.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.220
It is Kurzweil Applied
Intelligence Incorporation,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.490
where he has invented
voice command software.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.550
And finally, it's
Kurzweil Music System,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.980
where he has invented a lot of
professional musicians' systems

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.620
for computers.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.900
And so I was actually
impressed, already,

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.260
by that list of companies,
but was even more impressed

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.590
because his innovative
technology is not the only part

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.930
of why he is so much accepted.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.780
But he's also very much
accepted within academia

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.770
because his contributions to
computer science and academia

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.280
are enormous.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.750
And so he got no less than
nine honorary doctorate degrees

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.870
in science, and
engineering, and in music,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.580
received numerous awards--

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.250
and I only mentioned two.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.480
One is the Inventor
of the Year at MIT,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.670
and the other one is that he
received the award for the Most

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.450
Outstanding Computer Science
Book of 1990 for his book

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.820
The Age of Intelligent
Machines, which was

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.940
published in 1990 by MIT Press.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.380
I can highly
recommend that book.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.880
It's very fun to read.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.510
And he is currently working on
another book, which is called

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.400
The Age of Spiritual Machines.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.260
And I'm very, very
happy that you are here

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.840
today to share with us the
thoughts about this book.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.340
Welcome.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.730
KURZWEIL: My wife is
annoyed that I skipped

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.900
the age of emotional
machines, but we'll

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.150
try to cover that as well.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.610
It's a pleasure to be here
my old stomping grounds.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:41.840
When I was a student
here, we had one IBM 1794

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.900
which was shared by
several thousand students

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:45.550
and professors.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.690
You needed some influence to get
more than a few seconds a day.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:54.110
And it ran at a quarter of
a MIP and had 32K of RAM.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:59.060
Actually, core memory
it was back then.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.240
So things have progressed
a bit, actually

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.430
in lock-step to
Moore's law, which

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.110
we'll talk a little bit about.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:12.780
Let me start with a story that
some of you may recognize.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.149
But I think for a lot of you,
it might be before your time.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:20.510
"The gambler had not
expected to be here.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:22.340
But on reflection,
he thought, yes, he

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.160
had shown some
kindness in his time.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.190
And this place was even more
beautiful and satisfying

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.690
than he had imagined.

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.580
Everywhere there were
magnificent crystal

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.780
chandeliers, the finest handmade
carpets, the most sumptuous

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.560
foods, and, yes, the most
beautiful women who seemed

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.990
intrigued with their
new heaven-mate.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.600
He tried his hand at roulette
and, amazingly, his number

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.490
came up time after time.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.370
He tried the gaming
tables and his luck

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.060
was nothing short of remarkable,
winning game after game.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.370
Indeed, his winnings were
causing quite a stir,

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.400
attracting much excitement
by the attentive staff

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.290
and by the beautiful women.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.450
This continued day after
day, week after week,

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.260
with the gambler
winning every game,

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.750
accumulating bigger
and bigger winnings.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:11.820
Everything was going his way.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.160
He just kept on
winning and winning.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.170
And week after week,
and month after month,

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.920
the gambler's winning
streak seemed unbreakable.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.460
After a while this
started to get tedious.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.460
The gambler was
getting agitated.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.200
The winning was starting
to lose its meaning.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.130
But nothing changed.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:32.060
He just kept on
winning every game,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.150
until one day, the now
anguished gambler turned

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.370
to the angel who
seemed to be in charge

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.650
and said that he
couldn't take it anymore.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.120
Heaven was not
for him after all.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.840
He had really figured he was
destined for the other place

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.250
after all.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:49.170
And indeed, that's
where he wanted to be.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:53.500
But this is the other
place, came the reply."

00:05:53.500 --> 00:06:00.494
That's my recollection of an
episode of the Twilight Zone

00:06:00.494 --> 00:06:01.660
that I saw as a young child.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.319
Anybody here happen
to see that episode?

00:06:05.319 --> 00:06:07.360
I think the title might
have been something like,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.740
be careful what you wish for.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.980
Or at least that's
what I would call it.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.630
And as this engaging
series was wont to do,

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:18.350
it illustrated many of the
paradoxes of human nature.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.910
We like to solve problems, but
we don't want them all solved.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.860
Not too quickly, anyway.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:26.880
We're more attached to the
problems than the solutions--

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.440
maybe to the process
of solving them.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.710
Take death, for example.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.540
A great deal of our effort
goes into avoiding it.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.310
We make extraordinary efforts
to delay it and, indeed,

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:39.420
often consider its
intrusion as a tragic event.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:42.310
Yet we would find it
hard to live without it.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.290
Death gives meaning
to our lives.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.170
It gives importance
and value to time.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:50.050
Time would become meaningless
if there were too much of it.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.960
If death were indefinitely
put off or even just put off

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.390
for a very long
time in human terms,

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.590
the human psyche would end up--

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.700
well, like the gambler
in the Twilight Zone.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.190
Now, we don't yet
have this predicament.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.270
We have no shortage today of
either death or human problems.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:10.090
Few observers feel that the
20th century has left us

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.190
with too much of a good thing.

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.730
There is growing prosperity
fueled, not incidentally,

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.950
by information technology.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.930
But the human species
is still challenged

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:24.120
with issues and difficulties not
altogether different than those

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.590
it has struggled with from
the beginning of our recorded

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.140
history.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:32.630
The 21st century will be
different, in my view.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:36.110
The human species, along with
the computational technology

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:39.080
that it initiated
the creation of,

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:43.070
will solve age-old problems
of need, if not desire.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:46.930
And we'll be in a position to
change the nature of mortality

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.340
in a post-biological future.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.800
I'll explain what I mean
about that a little bit later.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.150
Now, do we have the
psychological capacity for all

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:58.550
the good things that await us?

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:02.360
Probably not, but that
might change as well.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:05.330
Before the next century
is over, human beings

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:08.750
will no longer be the most
intelligent or capable type

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:11.540
of entity on the planet.

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.250
Now, as the abstract
pointed out,

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:15.800
let me take that
controversial statement back.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.730
The truth of that statement
depends on how we define human,

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:23.360
and that will be a key
issue in the next century.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.160
Unlike the 20th
century, the primary

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.010
political and
philosophical issue

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.920
will be the definition
of who we are.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:33.740
But I'm getting a little
bit of ahead of myself.

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.250
To approach the 21st century, we
need to start from the present.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:42.020
If we jump to the endgame, it's
a little unsettling and maybe

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.540
not quite believable.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.100
But if we take it
step by step, I

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:50.040
think we'll see the inexorable
logic of the next century.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.390
This last century has seen
enormous technological change

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.420
and the social upheavals
that go along with it, which

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.700
few pundits circa-1899 foresaw.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.010
And the pace of change
is accelerating,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:06.040
and it has been since the
inception of invention.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.420
And as we will discuss,
this acceleration

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.970
is an inherent
feature of technology.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.550
That's what makes
technology different from

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:14.930
just simply the use of tools.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:16.680
There are a lot of
animals that use tools,

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:18.770
or at least there are
some, but those tools

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.810
don't evolve with time.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.850
Our tools evolve.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:25.070
The genes of that
evolution are really

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.950
our own knowledge base, which
we use to advance technology.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.205
And that's an inherent
nature of technology.

00:09:31.205 --> 00:09:33.710
And the result will be far
greater transformations

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.050
in the first two decades
of the 21st century

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.770
than we saw in the
entire 20th century.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.300
But to appreciate
the logic of this,

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.610
we have to go back and
start with the present.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.920
Now, computers today
exceed human intelligence

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.510
in a broad variety
of intelligence

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.170
yet very narrow domains, such
as playing chess, diagnosing

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:58.100
certain medical conditions,
timing buy-and-sell decisions,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.740
guiding cruise
missiles, and so on.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.880
Yet human intelligence remains
far more supple and flexible.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.440
Computers are still
unable to tell

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.859
the difference between
a dog and a cat,

00:10:08.859 --> 00:10:11.150
although I-- actually, I've
been saying that for years.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:14.750
I think we probably could
program a neural net today

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.000
to make that distinction.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.100
But computers can't tie
a pair of shoelaces,

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:23.930
they can't describe the objects
on a crowded kitchen table,

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:27.350
write a summary of a
movie, recognize humor,

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:30.890
or provide other subtle tasks
in which their human creators

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:32.380
excel.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.300
Now, one reason for this
disparity in capabilities

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.850
is that our most
advanced computers still

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:40.340
remain far simpler devices
than the human brain

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.686
by a factor of
approximately a million,

00:10:43.686 --> 00:10:45.560
give or take a couple
of orders of magnitude,

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.890
depending on what
assumptions you use.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:49.710
But computers, as
is well known, have

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:53.822
been doubling in speed and
capacity or complexity--

00:10:53.822 --> 00:10:55.280
which, it's important
to point out,

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:59.600
is actually a quadrupling
of computational ability--

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.370
every 18 months
since the inception

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:04.790
of calculating devices at
the beginning of the century.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:06.200
And this trend will continue.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.930
I'll come back to
Moore's law in a moment.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:12.170
But if we extrapolate
that out, computers

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:16.160
will achieve the computing speed
and capacity of the human brain

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:18.920
by around the year 2020.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.610
Now of course, achieving the
basic complexity and capacity

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.890
of the human brain
will not automatically

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:28.100
result in computers matching
the flexibility and range

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.690
of human intelligence.

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:32.960
The organization and
content of these resources

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.970
that is the software
of intelligence

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:36.740
is equally important.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:40.670
And in a few minutes, I'll talk
about at least one scenario

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:43.620
that I think is credible
to achieve this.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:46.710
Once a computer achieves a
human level of intelligence,

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.850
it will necessarily
roar past it.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.820
Since inception, computers
have been far more powerful

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:55.010
than computers in
memory and speed.

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.100
The computer can remember
billions or trillions of facts

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.800
with extreme reliability,
can extract information

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.720
from billions of
items in a database

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.310
in a matter of seconds.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.970
We're hard pressed to remember
a handful of phone numbers

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.750
or the directions to get here.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:14.540
The combination of human-level
intelligence in a machine--

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.130
with the fact that
electronic circuits

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:19.040
are a million times faster
than neural circuits--

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.950
combined with the computer's
inherent superiority

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:24.470
in speed and
reliability of memory

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.135
will be a very
formidable combination.

00:12:27.135 --> 00:12:28.760
Now as I mentioned,
electronic circuits

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.490
are a million times faster
than neural circuits,

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:34.820
so once a computer achieves
the human level of ability

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.560
in understanding
abstract concepts,

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.830
recognizing patterns, the
ability to read, for example,

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.470
it'll be able to apply
this ability to a knowledge

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.715
base of all human
acquired knowledge.

00:12:44.715 --> 00:12:46.880
In fact, there's
a project in Kyoto

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.664
at ATR, Advanced
Technology Research

00:12:49.664 --> 00:12:51.080
Laboratory, where
they're building

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:55.610
a billion neuron neural net.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.190
And they plan to teach
it the ability to read.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:00.530
And then we'll
basically set it loose

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.740
on all published human
literature, at least that's

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.160
available electronic form.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.210
Now, a common reaction
to the proposition

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:12.500
that computers will seriously
compete with human intelligence

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.430
is to dismiss this specter based
primarily on an examination

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:18.710
of contemporary capability.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:20.360
I mean after all,
when you interact

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:22.970
with your personal
computer, its intelligence

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:26.837
seems limited and brittle, if
it seems intelligent at all.

00:13:26.837 --> 00:13:28.670
It's hard to imagine
one's personal computer

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:31.700
having a sense of humor,
or holding an opinion,

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:34.580
or displaying any of the
other endearing qualities

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.470
of human intelligence.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.750
But the state of the art
in computer technology

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:40.820
is anything but static.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.410
Computer capabilities
are emerging

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:44.840
today that were considered
impossible one or two

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.700
decades ago.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:50.510
Examples include the ability
to accurately transcribe

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:54.170
normal continuous speech, which
all three leading companies

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:55.370
in speech recognition--

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:58.790
Dragon, my own company, Kurzweil
Applied Intelligence, and IBM--

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.010
recently demonstrated at COMDEX.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.770
The ability to intelligently
respond to natural language,

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.080
to recognize patterns
in medical tests,

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.480
such as electrocardiograms
and blood cell

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.690
tests with an accuracy
exceeding human physicians.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:15.210
And of course, to play chess
at a world championship level.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:18.500
In the next decade, we'll
see translating telephones.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:20.250
My company, Kurzweil
Applied Intelligence,

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.130
was recently acquired by
Lernout and Hauspie that has

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:24.480
language translation software.

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.563
And we're putting together
a translating telephone

00:14:26.563 --> 00:14:28.340
with continuous
speech recognition,

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.080
text-to-text
language translation,

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:32.700
and text-to-speech synthesis.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:36.450
And we plan to hold a
demonstration early in 1998,

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:38.610
where you'll be able to
call up somebody in Germany

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:42.270
and hold a conversation
in real time

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:46.062
with your speech translating
to German and vice versa.

00:14:46.062 --> 00:14:47.770
In the second decade
of the next century,

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:50.230
it'll become increasingly
difficult to draw

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.140
any clear distinction between
the capabilities of human

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:55.270
and machine intelligence.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.570
Evolution has been seen as a
billion-year drama that led

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:01.630
inexorably to its
grandest creation--

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:03.430
human intelligence.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:05.410
The emergence in the
early 21st century

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.000
of a new form of
intelligence that

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.180
can compete with and,
in some arenas at least,

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:12.310
exceed that of
human intelligence

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:14.770
will be, in my view, a
development of greater import

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:18.400
than anything we saw
in the 20th century.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:20.850
Now, this specter
is not yet here.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.400
Computers today are still
a great deal simpler

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.062
than the human brain.

00:15:25.062 --> 00:15:26.520
But with the
emergence of computers

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.810
that really rival and exceed
the human brain in complexity

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:33.210
will come a corresponding
ability of machines

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:38.010
to understand and respond to
abstractions and to subtleties.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.680
Human beings appear to be
complex in part because

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.950
of their competing
internal goals--

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:48.240
things we call values,
emotions, represent and engender

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.890
subgoals that often
conflict and, in my view,

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:54.600
are an unavoidable
byproduct of the complexity

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.430
that is human nature.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.840
As computers achieve a
comparable level of complexity

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:02.880
and as they are increasingly
derived from models

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:05.640
of human intelligence,
they too will necessarily

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.420
utilize goals that appear to
exhibit values and emotions--

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:11.790
of course, not always the
same values and emotions

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:13.530
that humans exhibit.

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:17.070
And a variety of philosophical
issues will emerge.

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.290
Are computers thinking or
are they just calculating?

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:22.470
Conversely, are human
beings thinking or are we

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.120
just calculating?

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:26.670
Presumably our brains
follow the laws of physics

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:30.540
so they must be machines,
albeit very complex ones.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:33.870
Is there an inherent difference
between machine thinking

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.940
and human thinking?

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:37.500
To put it another
way, once computers

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:39.330
are as complex as
the human brain

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.880
and can match the human
brain in the subtlety

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:46.240
and complexity of thought, are
we to consider them conscious?

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.200
It's a difficult
issue to even pose.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.920
Some philosophers feel it's
not a meaningful question.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.380
Other philosophers feel it's
the only meaningful question

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:56.940
in philosophy.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:59.160
And the question actually
goes back to Plato's time.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:01.785
They had debates on
this issue, and imagined

00:17:01.785 --> 00:17:03.660
machines of the complexity
of the human brain

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.880
made up of pistons, and
wires, and levers, and so on.

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.500
But as we actually have
machines that genuinely appear

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.920
to possess volition and
emotional responses,

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:16.802
this issue will become
increasingly compelling.

00:17:16.802 --> 00:17:18.490
Now let's talk for
a moment about what

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:21.099
it will take to achieve
just the hardware

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.849
capacity of the human brain.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:29.770
As is well known, computers have
been doubling in speed and also

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:32.200
the density of
computation, which

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:35.350
if you have a massively
parallel neural net

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:38.740
actually means you can put
twice as many neurons, run them

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:41.470
twice as fast, which is really
a quadrupling of the number

00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:43.570
of neural connection
calculations

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:47.054
you can do per second,
every 18 months.

00:17:47.054 --> 00:17:48.970
Computer memory today
is about a billion times

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:53.720
more powerful for the same cost
that it was when I was born.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.802
And if the automobile
industry had

00:17:55.802 --> 00:17:57.760
made as much progress in
the last half century,

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.100
cars today would cost
a hundredth of a cent

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.870
and go faster than
the speed of light.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:07.360
Now, this exponential progress
is called Moore's law.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.400
How many people here are
familiar with Moore's law?

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:13.970
I expect a lot of
hands to go up here.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:17.100
Intel chairman Gordon Moore
first commented on this,

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.200
actually commented
in the mid-'70s.

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:22.340
He said it was 24 months.

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.580
In the mid-'80s, he
revised that to 18 months.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.920
That's a corollary
of a broader law--

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:31.382
since nobody's claimed it,
I've named Kurzweil's law--

00:18:31.382 --> 00:18:34.580
on the exponentially
quickening pace of technology

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.400
that goes back to the
dawn of recorded history.

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:40.940
I mean, not much happened
in, say, the 10th century

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:42.990
technologically speaking.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:44.780
There may have been
one or two things.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:47.560
In the 18th century,
quite a bit happened.

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:49.420
In the 19th century,
a lot happened.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:52.730
And now in the 20th century,
we have major paradigm shifts

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:54.770
in a few years' time.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:56.480
Now, some observers
have commented

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:59.510
that exponential trends
can't go on forever.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:02.570
And we have lots of examples
of exponential trends

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:04.670
that go along and
then just hit a wall,

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:06.740
like a species happening
upon a new habitat,

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.900
is the classical example.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:12.680
Its numbers will multiply
geometrically for a time,

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:14.690
and then it hits a
wall and may even

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:20.000
go in reverse when it exhausts
the supply in its new habitat.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.540
But it would be highly premature
to predict the end of Moore's

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.620
law anytime soon.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.380
The chip companies
themselves have

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.750
said they can
confidently foresee

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:33.020
20 more years of
Moore's law, just

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.980
using conventional approaches.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.150
That alone is enough to put
the computational capacity

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:41.930
of the human brain into a
$1,000 personal computer.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:46.250
Moreover, today's chips are
essentially flat with only one

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:48.350
layer of circuitry,
whereas the brain

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.239
uses all three dimensions.

00:19:50.239 --> 00:19:51.780
We live in a
three-dimensional world.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.960
Why not use all
three dimensions?

00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:57.260
And circuits, particularly
superconducting circuits that

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:00.350
don't generate heat, will enable
the development of chips--

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:01.790
or I should say cubes--

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:03.920
with thousands of layers
of circuitry and also

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:06.080
smaller component
geometries, which

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.620
will multiply computing capacity
by a factor of millions.

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:11.720
There are more than enough
computing technologies

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:14.090
to assure the continuation
of Moore's law

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.800
for a very long time.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:19.530
Now, the human brain has
about 100 billion neurons.

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:22.140
On average, they have about a
thousand connections from one

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:22.950
neuron to the next.

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:27.090
That's 100 trillion connections.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:29.230
Each connection's
capable of a computation.

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:32.550
So that's a hundred
trillion-fold parallelism.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:34.650
These circuits are very slow.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:38.520
I mean, if we just copied the
human brain in electronic form,

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:40.730
it would run a
million times faster

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:45.000
because the neural circuits run
200 calculations per second.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.480
Typical electronic circuits, 200
million calculations a second.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:52.380
But 200 times 100 trillion is
20 million billion calculations

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:53.372
per second.

00:20:53.372 --> 00:20:55.080
And that's about what
you get in the year

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.000
2020 in a $1,000
personal computer

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.790
if you just track Moore's law.

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:04.890
And again, the chip companies
feel that they can do that just

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.040
with conventional
approaches, not even

00:21:08.040 --> 00:21:11.940
assuming things like crystalline
computers, and DNA computers,

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:15.190
and optical computers,
and quantum computers,

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:17.550
which is itself an interesting
subject because it's

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:19.310
a whole different
type of computation.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:23.600
But I won't get
sidetracked on that.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:26.470
Oh, and by the way, if
we were to harvest even

00:21:26.470 --> 00:21:28.630
a fraction of the unused
computes on the internet,

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:30.670
and there are some
proposals for doing

00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:34.300
that, we could create
virtual super computers

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:37.480
with the computing capacity
of the human brain today.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:40.500
Now, as we achieve each new
level of hardware capacity,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:43.060
new software technologies
become feasible.

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:46.170
And in fact, it becomes a
compelling economic pressure

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:50.710
to create software that matches
the hardware capability.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:52.710
And one of the exciting
things about Moore's law

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:55.400
is, every time that
that screw turns,

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:59.760
whole new business
opportunities become available.

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:04.500
As I mentioned, continuous
speech with natural language

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:06.550
understanding is now feasible.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:10.950
A product that Kurzweil Applied
Intelligence showed at COMDEX

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:16.382
recently, which got one of the
four best of COMDEX citations,

00:22:16.382 --> 00:22:18.840
allows you to say things like,
I enjoyed my trip to Belgium

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:19.779
last week.

00:22:19.779 --> 00:22:21.320
Make this paragraph
two points bigger

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:22.900
and change it's font to Arial.

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.062
I hope to go back
to Belgium soon.

00:22:25.062 --> 00:22:27.270
And it actually figures out
that that second sentence

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:29.250
is a command, and
then carries it out.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:33.810
And you can say the commands
in any way that you want.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.480
These systems require
200 megahertz Pentiums

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:38.610
with 32 megabytes.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:42.390
The point is that as hardware
capacity becomes available,

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.060
software capacities do follow.

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:46.800
And we will see
the same phenomena

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:50.040
with the full range of
human intellect, which

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.902
of course is comprised of many
different types of skills.

00:22:53.902 --> 00:22:55.860
Now, there are a number
of convincing scenarios

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:59.550
we could discuss, but let me
concentrate on just one which

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:02.460
I find particularly
compelling, which is reverse

00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:04.890
engineering the human brain.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:08.010
I mean, the design is
right there in front of us,

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:11.450
and it's not at all impossible
to get at the information.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:14.760
As the hardware capacity
to emulate the human brain,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.190
20 million billion calculations
per second-- and that's

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:18.810
conservatively high.

00:23:18.810 --> 00:23:22.000
I've seen other estimates that
are a thousand times lower.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:23.712
But as that capacity
becomes available--

00:23:23.712 --> 00:23:25.920
we're not there yet, but we
will be there in a couple

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:26.970
of decades--

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:30.150
projects to scan and understand
the algorithms of the brain

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.470
will be initiated.

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:33.090
In fact, that's
already been started.

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:38.430
Carver Mead's company,
Synaptics, basically studied

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:42.115
mammalian early vision--

00:23:42.115 --> 00:23:44.490
the neural organization
of mammalian early vision

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:48.060
by looking at cat cortexes.

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:52.230
And by examining the typology of
the interneuronal connections,

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:55.110
the algorithms become apparent.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:56.430
And it's not hard to see them.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:24:00.000
Now, these are not conventional,
sequential Von Neumann

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.070
algorithms, these are not
all digital algorithms.

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:05.790
These are hybrid
digital-analog algorithms.

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:07.920
They're massively parallel.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:13.480
They're not also conventional
neural net algorithms.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:15.410
They're specialized
neural net algorithms,

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:18.360
but you can see them by
examining the topology

00:24:18.360 --> 00:24:20.310
of the neural connections.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:23.530
And they created a chip
that, in fact, duplicates

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:25.840
mammalian early vision.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:29.340
Now, it's feasible, today,
to perform a destructive scan

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:30.960
of the human brain.

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:33.570
You could freeze a brain
and destructively scan it,

00:24:33.570 --> 00:24:38.370
and see all of the connections
of someone recently deceased,

00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:39.330
say.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:42.450
Now we might not want to base
our templates of intelligence

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:46.200
on someone who's
recently deceased.

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:49.560
Better to catch someone
before they died.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:51.870
Recently, a serial killer
agreed to be scanned

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:55.440
before he was executed, and
you can buy all 500 megabytes

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:58.070
of him on a CD-ROM.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:01.230
This is available.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:04.380
We may not want to base
our designs on the mind

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:06.690
of a serial killer, either.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:08.890
But in terms of
noninvasive scanning,

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:13.590
magnetic resonance imaging today
can image individual somas,

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.030
or nerve cell bodies.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:18.450
We can see individual
cells with today's MRI.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.540
And the limitation of the
resolution and speed of MRI

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:23.530
is the speed of computation.

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:25.050
So as computers get faster--

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:28.470
MRI is a very computationally
intensive process--

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:32.000
our MRIs can be high resolution.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.590
And in about 10 years--

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:38.730
in fact, the design of
MRIs now being designed,

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:42.000
which will be in the market
in four or five years,

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:46.530
can see the individual
connections.

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:49.980
The axons, dendrites, synapses,
and other neural components

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:52.680
that make up the
interneuronal wiring.

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:56.340
It will be feasible to
actually reverse engineer

00:25:56.340 --> 00:26:00.540
these connections with
MRI in about 10 years.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.040
Eventually, we'll
be able to resolve

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:05.160
the presynaptic vesicles
that many scientists believe

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:07.430
is the site of human learning.

00:26:07.430 --> 00:26:09.830
Just as the Human Genome
Project is providing us

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:12.830
with the templates of
the human genetic system,

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:15.620
efforts to scan the neural
organization of the brain

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:18.062
will provide us with the
templates of intelligence.

00:26:18.062 --> 00:26:19.520
And I will point
out that there was

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:24.470
a lot of skepticism about the
Genome Project 20 years ago.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:28.670
And I mentioned this
project in ATR in Kyoto,

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:30.470
where they are in
fact now building--

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.220
expect to have this
in a couple of years--

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:34.910
a billion neuron neural net.

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:39.050
Implemented in hardware, not one
of these software neural nets.

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:40.880
And that's actually
pretty competitive with

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:43.910
the human brain right there.

00:26:43.910 --> 00:26:46.150
Now let's talk a little
bit about the impact

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:51.130
of the emergence of a
thinking and feeling machine.

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:53.120
In this book The Age of
Intelligent Machines,

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:55.780
which I wrote 10
years ago, I predicted

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:58.510
that a computer would
be world chess champion

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:00.830
before the turn of the century.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:02.720
And I went on to say
that when this happened,

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:05.870
we would either think
more of computers,

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:10.700
less of human intelligence,
or less of chess.

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:14.240
And if history was a guide,
we would think less of chess.

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:18.230
And indeed, that's
exactly what we're seeing.

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:20.500
Observers from Doug
Hofstadter, to People Magazine,

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:23.150
to Scientific American
have discovered

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:26.120
that chess is not so intelligent
and creative an endeavor,

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:28.040
after all.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:31.070
Rather, it's really a matter
of brute force computation,

00:27:31.070 --> 00:27:33.350
for which computers
are well suited.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:36.410
But continuing to defend the
superiority of human intellect

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:40.040
in this way as we go through
the next couple of decades

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:42.150
will become
increasingly difficult.

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:46.020
A competition from intelligent
machines may seem threatening,

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:48.950
so one might expect constraints
of one kind or another

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:51.950
to be placed on this phenomenon
while the human species is

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:53.700
still in charge.

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:57.230
But while the overall phenomenon
may appear to be unsettling,

00:27:57.230 --> 00:28:00.590
defining and implementing
any specific limitations

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:02.630
will not be at all
straightforward,

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.150
and I don't think
will be feasible.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:07.190
The survival on Earth
of intelligent entities

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:11.600
that rival human thinking
is not an alien invasion.

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:15.170
Thinking machines are
humankind's own creation,

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:18.350
and evolution of tools intended
for our own productivity

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:19.790
and efficiency.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:21.860
And indeed, we're
already enormously

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:23.960
dependent on our
intelligent machines, just

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:25.790
the contemporary genre.

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:27.740
If all our computers
were to cease operating,

00:28:27.740 --> 00:28:30.170
society could hardly function.

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:32.690
The drive to add intelligence
and knowledge to our computing

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:36.890
devices is fueled by a
compelling economic incentive.

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:38.450
We've entered an
age of knowledge

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:42.110
in which the knowledge content
of products and services

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:45.200
is asymptoting to
100% of their value.

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:46.760
The enormous market
capitalization

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:48.410
of the computer industry--

00:28:48.410 --> 00:28:51.800
one recent estimate put the
market cap of just Silicon

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:54.800
Valley companies started
in the last 10 years

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:57.361
at approximately $1 trillion.

00:28:57.361 --> 00:28:59.360
And that's really where
most of the wealth today

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.100
is being created.

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:03.000
There was another
large figure from MIT,

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.980
but it was actually lower
than Stanford's number.

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:07.305
I'm not sure why that is.

00:29:07.305 --> 00:29:09.410
Might be the warm weather there.

00:29:09.410 --> 00:29:11.960
But a computerized
system that possesses

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:14.900
more flexible intelligence
and greater content

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:17.270
renders its
competition obsolete.

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:19.100
Stopping this
process would require

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:23.045
repealing this basic engine
of economic competition.

00:29:23.045 --> 00:29:26.120
Now, it may appear that we have
full control of our machines

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:28.430
today, yet computers
already make

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:30.200
the majority of
buy-and-sell decisions

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:32.630
in our financial markets,
assemble products,

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:35.060
evaluate the work
product of human workers,

00:29:35.060 --> 00:29:37.040
render medical
judgments and diagnoses,

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:40.130
guide airplanes, and conduct
a myriad of other tasks

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:45.106
on which we depend and by
which we are deeply affected.

00:29:45.106 --> 00:29:46.730
And that's with
machines that are still

00:29:46.730 --> 00:29:50.480
a million times simpler
today than the human brain.

00:29:50.480 --> 00:29:54.380
When machines progress to be
as complex and ultimately more

00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:56.480
complex than the
human brain, which

00:29:56.480 --> 00:29:59.090
will happen in the next
century, our dependence

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:00.710
will only deepen.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.380
This process is
gradual and ongoing.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:07.686
It's a seamless progression
with no obvious discontinuities.

00:30:07.686 --> 00:30:10.970
Now, computers don't yet
appear to be conscious players

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:12.297
in this drama.

00:30:12.297 --> 00:30:13.880
No one wondered what
Deep Blue thought

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:18.200
of its own victory or Gary
Kasparov's press conference.

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:20.600
It's brilliant at playing
chess but it's otherwise

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:24.080
an idiot savant with
apparently little ability

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:26.800
to register opinions
or emotions.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:29.060
No on worries today about
causing pain and suffering

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:31.320
to their computer programs.

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:36.650
But all of this will change,
not suddenly but inexorably,

00:30:36.650 --> 00:30:38.370
nonetheless.

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:41.160
And we can address these
issues by simply assuming

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.790
that the human-machine
nexus represents strictly

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:46.932
an us-them relationship.

00:30:46.932 --> 00:30:48.390
Remember that
computers are already

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:51.781
deeply embedded in the
grain of our civilization.

00:30:51.781 --> 00:30:54.030
And this relationship will
ultimately become much more

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:56.160
intimate than it is today.

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:59.880
We have today artificial
skin, bones, joints, hearts,

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:02.520
blood vessels,
pancreatic implants.

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:06.150
So why not replace at least some
of our slow, unreliable, and

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.730
aging neural circuits
with faster, reliable,

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:10.200
synthetic ones?

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:12.150
We already have
cochlear implants.

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:14.790
Why not implants to
improve vision, memory,

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:16.320
and reasoning speed?

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:18.000
Why stop there?

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:19.980
These things sound
controversial.

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:22.054
Of course, there was an
article just in today's--

00:31:22.054 --> 00:31:23.470
I think was today's
New York Times

00:31:23.470 --> 00:31:27.360
that human cloning
was considered very

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:31.410
controversial some weeks ago
and now people are saying, well,

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:33.820
why not?

00:31:33.820 --> 00:31:36.420
So views of these
things do change.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:38.070
The computers of
the 21st century

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:41.070
will appear to have human-like
emotions because, in at least

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:42.870
some cases, their
designs will have

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:46.880
been derived from the reverse
engineering of human circuits.

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:48.900
And like humans,
they will have--

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:50.700
or at least appear to have--

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:52.530
spiritual experiences.

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:55.470
They will wonder, or at
least profess to wonder,

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.027
about the true nature of
consciousness and free will.

00:31:58.027 --> 00:31:59.610
They'll wonder if
it's really possible

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:02.910
that humans are capable of being
aware of their own existence,

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.470
or if we only act as if we are.

00:32:05.470 --> 00:32:08.220
And that's not adequate to
state that such developments

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:12.510
won't occur because of ethical
or political proscriptions.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:14.340
The age of very
intelligent machines

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:16.470
will not be a single phenomenon.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.750
Human ethics will
face a multiplicity

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.500
of slippery slopes.

00:32:21.500 --> 00:32:24.650
Now, the advent of machines
that match and ultimately exceed

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:26.960
the full range of
human intellect

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:29.150
won't arrive on a single day.

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:32.420
It will be a seamless,
ever-accelerating progression.

00:32:32.420 --> 00:32:35.390
And to get a feel for
how this will unfold,

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:38.810
I'd like to consider some
milestones along the way.

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:41.110
Now with the
impending millennium,

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:42.610
there are no shortage
of predictions

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:46.150
about what the next century
will be like, and futurism

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:48.310
itself has a long history.

00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:50.674
Not a very impressive one.

00:32:50.674 --> 00:32:52.840
One of the problems with
anticipations of the future

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:54.760
is that by the
time it's apparent

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:57.160
that these predictions
have had little resemblance

00:32:57.160 --> 00:33:00.562
to future events, it's too
late to get your money back.

00:33:00.562 --> 00:33:02.770
Fortunately, you didn't have
to pay for this lecture.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:09.100
Let me start with a sampling
of some of the predictions

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:13.080
I made 10 years ago
about the 1990s.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:15.700
Of course, I picked
out the better ones.

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:19.810
But if you buy the book,
you can see them all.

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:23.940
But in 1988, I wrote, "Computer
will defeat the world chess

00:33:23.940 --> 00:33:26.440
champion before the year 2000,
and we'll think less of chess

00:33:26.440 --> 00:33:29.170
as a result. There'll
be a sustained decline

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:32.320
in the value of commodities,
i.e. material resources,

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.780
with most new wealth being
created in the knowledge

00:33:34.780 --> 00:33:36.670
contents of products
and services,

00:33:36.670 --> 00:33:39.670
leading to sustained economic
growth and prosperity."

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:41.920
And interestingly,
Fed Chairman Greenspan

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:45.700
did recently acknowledge
that today's unprecedented,

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:48.520
sustained prosperity
and economic expansion

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:51.100
is due to the increased
efficiency provided

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:52.980
by information technology.

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:55.450
Occasionally you
read studies that

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:58.510
say that there's no
increase of productivity

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:00.489
from information technology.

00:34:00.489 --> 00:34:02.530
The problem with these
studies is they factor out

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:03.196
the improvement.

00:34:03.196 --> 00:34:05.590
They put it in both the
numerator and the denominator,

00:34:05.590 --> 00:34:08.590
and say, we still only get
$10 of work done for $10.

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:11.840
And we only get an hour
of work done in an hour,

00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:13.449
because we expect
to do a lot more.

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:17.000
There's no question that
productivity has increased.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:19.719
In my companies, two people
can handle the operations

00:34:19.719 --> 00:34:23.139
that used to require 15 or 20
people before we had computers

00:34:23.139 --> 00:34:25.840
to organize these things.

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:27.760
But Greenspan is
really only half right.

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:30.639
He ignores the fact that most
of the new wealth being created

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:33.610
is, itself, comprised of
information and knowledge--

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:36.090
$1 trillion in
Silicon Valley alone.

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:38.989
Increased efficiency is
only part of the story.

00:34:38.989 --> 00:34:41.080
"A worldwide information
network linking

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:42.610
almost all
organizations and tens

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:45.429
of millions of individuals,"
admittedly not by the name

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:46.750
World Wide Web.

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:49.182
"In warfare, almost total
reliance and digital imaging,

00:34:49.182 --> 00:34:51.390
pattern recognition, and
other software technologies.

00:34:51.390 --> 00:34:54.329
The side with the smarter
machines will win.

00:34:54.329 --> 00:34:55.870
The vast majority
of commercial music

00:34:55.870 --> 00:34:58.570
will be created on
computer-based synthesizers.

00:34:58.570 --> 00:35:01.380
With the advent of widespread
electronic communication,

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:02.890
in the Soviet Union
uncontrollable

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:04.780
political forces
will be unleashed.

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:08.020
These will be methods far
more powerful than the copiers

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:09.915
the authorities have
traditionally banned.

00:35:09.915 --> 00:35:11.790
The authorities will be
unable to control it.

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:13.600
Totalitarian control
of information

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:15.250
will have been broken.

00:35:15.250 --> 00:35:17.380
Continuous speech
recognition for dictation."

00:35:17.380 --> 00:35:19.510
Although, I did
have that in 1993.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:22.950
So we were off by a few years.

00:35:22.950 --> 00:35:25.160
"And then by the year 2000,
chips with over a billion

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:27.260
transistors."

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:29.960
So let me share
with you a sampling

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:32.510
of some predictions
for particular points

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:36.110
in time in the next century.

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:38.840
And these are predictions
I'm developing for the sequel

00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:41.060
to the Age of Intelligent
Machines, which

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:44.700
is a book with the same
title as this lecture.

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.490
Start with 2009,
the computer itself.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:49.890
"The majority of text is
created using continuous speech

00:35:49.890 --> 00:35:52.770
recognition, but
keyboards are still used.

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:55.710
Also ubiquitous are LUIS,
language user interfaces,

00:35:55.710 --> 00:35:59.550
using continuous speech and
natural language understanding.

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:03.550
Computer displays have the
display qualities of paper.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:06.600
Chips are three-dimensional
with many layers of circuitry.

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:09.030
Supercomputers match at
least the hardware capacity

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:10.650
of the human brain.

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:12.270
Unused computes on
the internet are

00:36:12.270 --> 00:36:15.570
being harvested, creating
human brain-capacity, virtual,

00:36:15.570 --> 00:36:17.820
parallel supercomputers.

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:20.520
There's increasing interest in
massively parallel neural nets,

00:36:20.520 --> 00:36:24.060
genetic algorithms, and other
forms of chaotic or complexity

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:27.000
theory computing, although
the majority of computes

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:29.100
of computers still use
conventional sequential

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:30.660
processing.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:32.370
Research has been
initiated on reverse

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:35.032
engineering the human brain.

00:36:35.032 --> 00:36:36.490
Education-- the
majority of reading

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:39.100
is done on displays, although
the installed base of paper

00:36:39.100 --> 00:36:41.350
documents is still formidable.

00:36:41.350 --> 00:36:43.744
Students of all ages routinely
have their own computer,

00:36:43.744 --> 00:36:45.160
which is a very
thin slab weighing

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:46.990
about a pound with a
very high resolution

00:36:46.990 --> 00:36:49.480
display suitable for reading.

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:51.100
Intelligent
courseware has emerged

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:53.470
as a primary means of learning.

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:55.240
Learning is becoming
a significant portion

00:36:55.240 --> 00:36:57.190
of most jobs.

00:36:57.190 --> 00:36:59.770
Learning at a distance
is commonplace.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:01.780
Translating telephone
technology is commonly

00:37:01.780 --> 00:37:03.880
used for many language pairs.

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:06.060
Telephone communication
is primarily wireless

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:09.220
and routinely includes high
resolution moving images.

00:37:09.220 --> 00:37:11.320
Meetings of all kinds
and sizes routinely

00:37:11.320 --> 00:37:15.070
take place with geographically
separated participants.

00:37:15.070 --> 00:37:17.560
Haptic technologies allow
you to touch and feel objects

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:19.300
and people at a distance.

00:37:19.300 --> 00:37:21.430
People have sexual
experiences at a distance

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:25.270
with other persons, as well
as virtual partners, business,

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:26.830
and economics.

00:37:26.830 --> 00:37:30.040
Despite occasional corrections,
the 10 years leading up to 2009

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:34.090
has seen continuous economic
expansion and prosperity.

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:35.470
At least half of
all transactions

00:37:35.470 --> 00:37:37.150
are conducted online.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:39.610
Intelligence assistance--
which combine continuous speech

00:37:39.610 --> 00:37:42.820
recognition, natural language
understanding, problem solving,

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:44.770
and animated personalities--

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:47.110
routinely assist with finding
information, answering

00:37:47.110 --> 00:37:50.150
questions, and
conducting transactions.

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:52.270
A company west of
the Mississippi

00:37:52.270 --> 00:37:54.880
and north of the Mason-Dixon
line achieves $1 trillion

00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:56.698
in market capitalization."

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:01.560
I take no
responsibility for that

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:05.250
if you make
investment decisions.

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:07.140
"Political and social
issues-- privacy

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:09.654
has emerged as a
primary political issue.

00:38:09.654 --> 00:38:11.820
The virtual constant use
of electronic communication

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:13.920
technologies leaves a
highly detailed trail

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:15.810
of every person's move.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:19.320
There's a growing neo-Luddite
movement as the skill ladder--

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:21.900
i.e. the ladder,
level of skill needed

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:25.320
for meaningful employment--
continues to move upwards.

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:26.760
As with earlier
Luddite movements,

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:29.370
its influence is limited by
the level of prosperity made

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:31.380
possible by new technology.

00:38:31.380 --> 00:38:33.120
The movement does
succeed in establishing

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:35.970
continuing education as a
primary right associated

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.750
with employment.

00:38:37.750 --> 00:38:40.820
Musicians routinely jam
with cybernetic musicians.

00:38:40.820 --> 00:38:42.730
Philosophy-- there's
renewed interest

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:46.535
in the Turing Test," which I
won't describe, but many of you

00:38:46.535 --> 00:38:47.410
are familiar with it.

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:49.076
"Although computers
still fail the test,

00:38:49.076 --> 00:38:50.826
there's greater
confidence that they

00:38:50.826 --> 00:38:53.200
will be in a position to pass
the test within another one

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:54.710
or two decades."

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:58.390
This is a test for
seeing if computers

00:38:58.390 --> 00:39:01.330
have human equivalents
in intelligence,

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:03.990
at least one proposed
by Alan Turing.

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:05.860
"There's increasingly
serious speculation

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:08.140
on the potential
sentience or consciousness

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:10.014
of computer-based intelligence.

00:39:10.014 --> 00:39:11.680
The increasing
intelligence of computers

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:14.670
has spurred an interest
in philosophy."

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:18.860
Okay, now we'll jump to 2019,
about 20 years from now.

00:39:18.860 --> 00:39:22.490
"The computer itself-- computers
are now largely invisible.

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:25.100
They are embedded everywhere--
in tables, chairs, desks,

00:39:25.100 --> 00:39:27.582
clothing, jewelry, vehicles.

00:39:27.582 --> 00:39:29.540
People routinely use
three-dimensional displays

00:39:29.540 --> 00:39:31.070
built in their glasses.

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:33.020
Computer displays
create highly realistic

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:34.790
virtual, visual
environments overlaying

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:36.310
the real environment.

00:39:36.310 --> 00:39:38.540
This display technology
projects images directly

00:39:38.540 --> 00:39:40.610
onto the human
retina, and is widely

00:39:40.610 --> 00:39:42.950
used regardless of
visual impairment.

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:45.140
Keyboards are rare,
although they still exist.

00:39:45.140 --> 00:39:48.410
Most interaction with computing
is through physical gesture,

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:51.270
which computers respond to
both visually and physically,

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:53.150
and through spoken
communication.

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.790
Computers are facile with
two-way, natural language

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:57.264
communication.

00:39:57.264 --> 00:39:59.180
People communicate with
computers the same way

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:03.280
they would communicate
with a human assistant.

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:05.710
Significant attention is
paid to the personality

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:07.690
of computer-based
personal assistants,

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:10.130
with many choices available.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:13.580
Typically, people don't just own
one specific personal computer,

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:16.740
although computing is
nonetheless very personal.

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:19.480
The computational capacity
of $1,000 computing device

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:23.120
exceeds the computational
capability of the human brain.

00:40:23.120 --> 00:40:26.730
Of the total combined computing
capacity of the human species--

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:28.500
i.e. all human brains--

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:30.120
together with the
computing technology

00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:34.710
that the species has created,
more than 10% is non-human.

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:36.259
The majority of
computes of computers

00:40:36.259 --> 00:40:37.800
are now devoted to
massively parallel

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:40.080
neural nets and
genetic algorithms.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:41.760
Significant progress
has been made

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:44.910
in the scanning-based reverse
engineering of the human brain.

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:47.400
It's now fully recognized
that the brain is comprised

00:40:47.400 --> 00:40:50.490
of many specialized
regions, with the typology

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:52.560
and architecture of the
interneuronal connections

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:56.100
varying widely depending on the
region's information processing

00:40:56.100 --> 00:40:57.180
function.

00:40:57.180 --> 00:40:58.680
The massively
parallel algorithms

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:00.629
are beginning to be understood.

00:41:00.629 --> 00:41:02.670
To recognize that the
human genetic code does not

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:05.700
specify the sites in
interneuronal wiring of any

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:07.710
of these regions,
but rather sets up

00:41:07.710 --> 00:41:10.620
a rapid evolutionary
process in which connections

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:13.486
are established and
fight for survival.

00:41:13.486 --> 00:41:15.860
The standard process for wiring
machine-based neural nets

00:41:15.860 --> 00:41:19.100
uses a similar genetic
evolutionary algorithm.

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:21.230
Education-- most learning
is accomplished using

00:41:21.230 --> 00:41:23.270
intelligent virtual teachers.

00:41:23.270 --> 00:41:25.610
To the extent that teaching
is done by human teachers,

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:27.860
the human teachers are often
not in the local vicinity

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:29.102
of the student.

00:41:29.102 --> 00:41:30.560
The majority of
adult human workers

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:32.268
spend the majority of
their time learning

00:41:32.268 --> 00:41:33.730
new skills and knowledge.

00:41:33.730 --> 00:41:36.830
Communication-- you can do
virtually anything with anyone

00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:39.020
regardless of
physical proximity.

00:41:39.020 --> 00:41:40.400
The technology to
accomplish this

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:44.120
is easy to use and ever-present.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:45.997
Routinely available
technology includes

00:41:45.997 --> 00:41:48.080
high quality speech-to-speech
language translation

00:41:48.080 --> 00:41:51.350
for most common language pairs.

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:53.990
Business and economics--
rapid economic expansion

00:41:53.990 --> 00:41:55.970
and prosperity has continued.

00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:57.470
The vast majority
of transactions

00:41:57.470 --> 00:41:59.000
include a simulated
person, which

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:02.150
includes a realistic animated
personality and two-way voice

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.810
communication with high quality
natural language understanding.

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:07.515
Political and social issues--

00:42:07.515 --> 00:42:09.890
people are beginning to have
relationships with automated

00:42:09.890 --> 00:42:13.040
personalities and use them
as companions, teachers,

00:42:13.040 --> 00:42:14.870
caretakers, and lovers.

00:42:14.870 --> 00:42:17.030
Automated personalities
are superior to humans

00:42:17.030 --> 00:42:20.030
in some ways, such as having
very reliable memories

00:42:20.030 --> 00:42:24.059
and predictable personalities.

00:42:24.059 --> 00:42:25.850
They are not yet regarded
is equal to human

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:27.980
in the subtlety of
their personalities,

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:32.100
although there is
disagreement on this point.

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:34.490
An undercurrent of concern
is developing with regard

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:36.830
to the influence of
machine intelligence.

00:42:36.830 --> 00:42:39.080
There continues to be
differences between human

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:41.264
and machine intelligence,
but the advantages

00:42:41.264 --> 00:42:42.680
of human intelligence
are becoming

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.170
more difficult to
identify and articulate.

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:47.330
Computer intelligence
is thoroughly interwoven

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:49.820
into the mechanisms
of civilization,

00:42:49.820 --> 00:42:51.710
and is designed to be
outwardly subservient

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:54.320
to our apparent human control.

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:56.690
On the one hand, human
transactions and decisions

00:42:56.690 --> 00:43:00.390
require, by law, a human
agent of responsibility,

00:43:00.390 --> 00:43:02.961
even if fully initiated
by machine intelligence.

00:43:02.961 --> 00:43:04.460
On the other hand,
few decisions are

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:07.460
made without significant
involvement in consultation

00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:09.740
with machine-based intelligence.

00:43:09.740 --> 00:43:11.210
Virtual artists
and all of the arts

00:43:11.210 --> 00:43:13.640
are emerging and
are taken seriously.

00:43:13.640 --> 00:43:15.620
These virtual artists,
musicians, and authors

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:20.570
tend to be associated with
humans or organizations, which

00:43:20.570 --> 00:43:23.510
in turn are comprised of
collaborations of humans

00:43:23.510 --> 00:43:25.880
and machines who have
contributed to the knowledge

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:28.880
base and techniques of
these cybernetic artists.

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:31.490
However, interest in the output
of these creative machines

00:43:31.490 --> 00:43:35.750
has gone beyond the mere novelty
of machines being creative.

00:43:35.750 --> 00:43:38.550
Visual, musical, and literary
art created by human artists

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:40.970
is typically a collaboration
between human and machine

00:43:40.970 --> 00:43:42.320
intelligence.

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:44.240
The type of artistic
and entertainment object

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:49.120
in greatest demand continues to
be virtual experience software.

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:51.019
Philosophy-- there
are widespread reports

00:43:51.019 --> 00:43:53.560
of computers passing the Turing
Test, although these tests do

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:55.780
not meet the
criteria established

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:58.284
by knowledgeable observers.

00:43:58.284 --> 00:43:59.950
There is increasingly
serious discussion

00:43:59.950 --> 00:44:02.855
of the subjective experience
of computer-based intelligence,

00:44:02.855 --> 00:44:05.230
although discussion of the
rights of machine intelligence

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:07.989
is not yet in mainstream debate.

00:44:07.989 --> 00:44:10.030
Machine intelligence is
still largely the product

00:44:10.030 --> 00:44:12.700
of a collaboration of human
and machine intelligence,

00:44:12.700 --> 00:44:14.230
and has been
programmed to maintain

00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:18.370
a subservient relationship to
the species that created it."

00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:21.190
Okay, we'll jump to 2029.

00:44:21.190 --> 00:44:23.950
"Computer itself-- $1,000
unit of computation

00:44:23.950 --> 00:44:26.470
has a computing capacity
of approximately 10,000

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:27.800
human brains.

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:30.580
Of the total combined computing
capacity of the human species

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:32.620
along with the
computing technology

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:37.660
that it initiated the creation
of, more than 99% is non-human.

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:39.940
The vast majority of computes
of non-human computing

00:44:39.940 --> 00:44:42.610
is now massively parallel
neural nets, much of which

00:44:42.610 --> 00:44:45.720
is based on the reverse
engineering of the human brain.

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:47.970
Many, but less than a
majority, of the specialized

00:44:47.970 --> 00:44:50.400
regions of the human
brain have been decoded,

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:53.439
with their massively parallel
algorithms understood.

00:44:53.439 --> 00:44:55.230
The number of specialized
regions numbering

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:56.688
in the hundreds is
greater than was

00:44:56.688 --> 00:44:59.380
anticipated 20 years earlier.

00:44:59.380 --> 00:45:01.079
Displays are now
implanted in the eyes,

00:45:01.079 --> 00:45:02.620
with the choice of
permanent implants

00:45:02.620 --> 00:45:05.350
or removable implants
similar to contact lenses.

00:45:05.350 --> 00:45:07.330
Images are projected
directly onto the retina,

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:09.370
providing the usual
high resolution,

00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:13.120
three-dimensional display
overlaid on the physical world.

00:45:13.120 --> 00:45:14.680
Cochlear implants
originally used

00:45:14.680 --> 00:45:17.710
just for the hearing impaired
are now ubiquitously used.

00:45:17.710 --> 00:45:20.800
These implants provide both
auditory input and output

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:23.710
between the human user and the
worldwide computing network.

00:45:23.710 --> 00:45:25.510
Direct neural pathways
have been perfected

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:28.326
for direct high bandwidth
connection to the human brain.

00:45:28.326 --> 00:45:29.950
A range of neural
implants are becoming

00:45:29.950 --> 00:45:32.380
available to enhance visual
and auditory perception

00:45:32.380 --> 00:45:36.194
and interpretation,
memory, and reasoning.

00:45:36.194 --> 00:45:37.860
Human learning is
primarily accomplished

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:40.260
using virtual teachers
and enhanced by the widely

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:42.540
available neural implants.

00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:44.340
Automated agents are
learning on their own

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:47.186
without human spoon-feeding
of information and knowledge.

00:45:47.186 --> 00:45:48.810
Computers have read
all available human

00:45:48.810 --> 00:45:51.000
and machine
generated literature.

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:52.500
Significant new
knowledge is created

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:56.700
by machines with little
or no human intervention.

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:58.290
Human and non-human
intelligences

00:45:58.290 --> 00:45:59.910
are primarily focused
on the creation

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:03.030
of knowledge in its myriad forms
and their significant struggle

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:04.900
over intellectual
property rights,

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:08.130
including ever-increasing
levels of litigation.

00:46:08.130 --> 00:46:09.690
There is almost no
human employment

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:12.330
in production, agriculture,
and transportation.

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.402
The largest profession
is education.

00:46:14.402 --> 00:46:16.110
There are many more
lawyers than doctors.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:22.050
Computers appear to be passing
forms of the Turing Test deemed

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:24.450
valid by both human and
non-human authorities,

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:27.574
although controversy
on this point persists.

00:46:27.574 --> 00:46:29.740
There's not a sharp division
between the human world

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:31.210
and the machine world.

00:46:31.210 --> 00:46:34.090
Human cognition is being
ported to machines,

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:36.670
and many machines have
personalities, skills,

00:46:36.670 --> 00:46:39.520
and knowledge bases derived
from the reverse engineering

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:40.990
of human intelligence.

00:46:40.990 --> 00:46:43.330
Conversely, machine
intelligence-based neural

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:46.330
implants are providing enhanced
perceptual and cognitive

00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:48.180
functions to humans.

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:51.590
There's growing discussion on
what constitutes a human being.

00:46:51.590 --> 00:46:53.950
This is emerging as a
significant legal and political

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:55.810
issue.

00:46:55.810 --> 00:46:58.920
The rapidly growing capability
of machines is controversial,

00:46:58.920 --> 00:47:00.760
but there is no effective
resistance to it.

00:47:04.860 --> 00:47:06.570
Since machine
intelligence was initially

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:09.570
designed to be subservient
to you in control,

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:11.910
it has not presented
a threatening face

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:13.710
to the human population.

00:47:13.710 --> 00:47:15.795
Humans realize that
disengaging the now

00:47:15.795 --> 00:47:18.600
human-machine civilization
from its dependence on machine

00:47:18.600 --> 00:47:20.850
intelligence is not possible.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:23.790
There is growing discussion of
the legal rights of machines,

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:26.400
particularly machines that
are independent of humans.

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:28.260
Although not yet fully
recognized in law,

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:29.790
the pervasive
influence of machines

00:47:29.790 --> 00:47:31.800
in all levels of
decision making is

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:35.632
providing significant
protection to machines.

00:47:35.632 --> 00:47:38.090
Although computers routinely
pass valid forms of the Turing

00:47:38.090 --> 00:47:40.557
Test, there continues
to be controversy as to

00:47:40.557 --> 00:47:42.140
whether or not machine
intelligence is

00:47:42.140 --> 00:47:45.869
the equal of human intelligence
in all of its diversity.

00:47:45.869 --> 00:47:47.910
The subjective experience
of machine intelligence

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:50.550
is increasingly accepted,
particularly since machines

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:53.810
participate in this discussion.

00:47:53.810 --> 00:47:55.810
Machines claim to be
conscious, and these claims

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:57.924
are largely accepted.

00:47:57.924 --> 00:47:59.340
They claim to have
as wide a range

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:01.500
of emotional and
spiritual experiences

00:48:01.500 --> 00:48:04.100
as the human progenitors."

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:08.020
Okay, we'll jump
finally to year 2099.

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:11.060
"There's a strong trend towards
a merger of human cognition

00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:13.970
and perception with the world
of machine intelligence, which

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:16.721
the human species
initiated the creation of.

00:48:16.721 --> 00:48:18.470
The reverse engineering
of the human brain

00:48:18.470 --> 00:48:19.700
appears to be complete.

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:21.230
The hundreds of
specialized regions

00:48:21.230 --> 00:48:24.650
have been fully scanned,
analyzed, and understood.

00:48:24.650 --> 00:48:27.110
Machine analogs are based
on these human models, which

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:30.260
have been enhanced and extended
along with many other massively

00:48:30.260 --> 00:48:31.820
parallel algorithms.

00:48:31.820 --> 00:48:34.160
These enhancements combined
with the enormous advantages

00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:38.180
in speed and capacity of
electronic photonic circuits

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:42.450
provide substantial advantages
to machine-based intelligence.

00:48:42.450 --> 00:48:45.680
There is ubiquitous use of
neural-implant technology,

00:48:45.680 --> 00:48:48.050
which provides
enormous augmentation

00:48:48.050 --> 00:48:50.690
of human perceptual and
cognitive abilities.

00:48:50.690 --> 00:48:53.090
Humans who do not
utilize such implants

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:56.360
have difficulty engaging
in meaningful dialogues

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:58.570
with those who do.

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:01.060
There are machine-based
intelligences entirely derived

00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:03.024
from models of
human intelligence,

00:49:03.024 --> 00:49:04.690
although substantially
enhanced in terms

00:49:04.690 --> 00:49:07.180
of speed and processing,
memory capacity,

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:09.460
and perceptual and
cognitive ability.

00:49:09.460 --> 00:49:11.602
These intelligences
claim to be human,

00:49:11.602 --> 00:49:13.060
although their
brains are not based

00:49:13.060 --> 00:49:16.000
on carbon-based
cellular processes.

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:20.110
And there are many gradations
above these two scenarios.

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:21.760
The concept of what
it is to be human

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:23.889
has been significantly altered.

00:49:23.889 --> 00:49:25.930
The rights and powers of
different manifestations

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:27.880
of human and machine
intelligences

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:29.860
and their various
combinations represent

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:34.270
a primary political and
philosophical issue."

00:49:34.270 --> 00:49:39.760
Well, let me end this
lecture with just a couple

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:44.480
of philosophical
mind experiments.

00:49:44.480 --> 00:49:47.320
And these are experiments
that philosophers

00:49:47.320 --> 00:49:50.290
have thought about,
actually, long before we

00:49:50.290 --> 00:49:57.640
had the computational technology
to even conceivably implement

00:49:57.640 --> 00:50:01.270
these mind experiments.

00:50:01.270 --> 00:50:03.550
Well, consider the statement,
I am lonely and bored.

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:04.630
Please keep me company.

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:09.570
Now, if your computer
displayed that on its screen,

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.320
would that convince
you that your notebook

00:50:11.320 --> 00:50:13.980
is conscious and has feelings?

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:15.876
Well, clearly no.

00:50:15.876 --> 00:50:17.250
It's rather trivial
for a program

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:20.430
to display such a message,
and presumably the message

00:50:20.430 --> 00:50:24.000
comes from the human
author of the program.

00:50:24.000 --> 00:50:27.040
The computer is just a
conduit for the message,

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:29.380
much like a book or
a fortune cookie.

00:50:29.380 --> 00:50:32.860
Suppose we had speech
synthesis, and the computer now

00:50:32.860 --> 00:50:34.870
speaks its plaintive message.

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:36.375
Have we changed anything?

00:50:36.375 --> 00:50:40.120
Well, we've added some technical
complexity to the program,

00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:42.100
and some human-like
communication means,

00:50:42.100 --> 00:50:46.360
but we still don't regard the
computer as the genuine author

00:50:46.360 --> 00:50:47.950
of the message.

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:51.040
Suppose now that the message
is not explicitly programmed,

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:53.770
but it's produced by a
game-playing program that

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:57.130
contains a complex model
of its own situation.

00:50:57.130 --> 00:50:59.050
This specific message
may never have

00:50:59.050 --> 00:51:02.010
been foreseen by the human
authors of the program.

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:03.910
It's created by the
computer from the state

00:51:03.910 --> 00:51:09.430
of its own internal model as it
interacts with you, the user.

00:51:09.430 --> 00:51:12.340
Are we getting closer to
considering the computer

00:51:12.340 --> 00:51:15.600
as a conscious, feeling entity?

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:18.300
Well, maybe just a tad.

00:51:18.300 --> 00:51:20.400
But if we consider
contemporary game software,

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:22.230
this illusion is
probably short lived

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:25.740
as we run up against
the limitations

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:29.100
behind the computer's
ability for smalltalk.

00:51:29.100 --> 00:51:31.080
Now suppose the mechanisms
behind the message

00:51:31.080 --> 00:51:34.410
grow to become a massive
neural net built from silicon

00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:37.350
but based on the reverse
engineering of the human brain.

00:51:37.350 --> 00:51:39.000
Suppose we develop
a learning protocol

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:41.970
for the neural net that enables
it to learn human language

00:51:41.970 --> 00:51:43.680
and model human knowledge.

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:46.620
Its circuits are a million times
faster than human circuits,

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:49.320
so it has plenty of time to
read all human literature

00:51:49.320 --> 00:51:52.060
and develop its own
conceptions of reality.

00:51:52.060 --> 00:51:55.090
Its creators don't tell it
how to respond to the world.

00:51:55.090 --> 00:51:59.120
Suppose now that it
says, I'm lonely.

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:01.160
At what point do we
consider the computer

00:52:01.160 --> 00:52:04.400
to be a conscious agent
with its own free will?

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:07.610
And what exactly do
these words mean, anyway?

00:52:07.610 --> 00:52:12.350
This has been, really, the most
vexing problem in philosophy

00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:15.730
since the Platonic
dialogues illuminated

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:18.920
the inherent contradiction
we have in these terms.

00:52:18.920 --> 00:52:20.450
Let's consider
this slippery slope

00:52:20.450 --> 00:52:22.770
from the opposite direction.

00:52:22.770 --> 00:52:27.000
Our friend Jack-- circa
sometime in the 21st century--

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:29.730
has been complaining
lately of some difficulty

00:52:29.730 --> 00:52:31.330
with his hearing.

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:33.720
A diagnostic test
indicates he needs more

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:37.740
than a conventional hearing aid
so he gets a cochlear implant.

00:52:37.740 --> 00:52:40.740
Once used only by people with
severe hearing impairments,

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:43.620
these implants are now common
to correct the ability of people

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:45.390
to hear across the
entire sonic spectrum.

00:52:48.580 --> 00:52:51.490
The surgical procedure
is successful,

00:52:51.490 --> 00:52:54.670
and Jack is pleased with
his improved hearing.

00:52:54.670 --> 00:52:57.580
Is this still the same person?

00:52:57.580 --> 00:52:59.110
Well, sure he is.

00:52:59.110 --> 00:53:02.620
People have cochlear
implants today, in 1997.

00:53:02.620 --> 00:53:05.140
We still regard them
as the same person.

00:53:05.140 --> 00:53:08.350
Now Jack is so impressed with
the success of his cochlear

00:53:08.350 --> 00:53:10.660
implants that he likes
to switch on the built

00:53:10.660 --> 00:53:12.520
in phonics-cognition circuits.

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:14.570
And these circuits
are already built in,

00:53:14.570 --> 00:53:17.290
so in case he
decides to use them

00:53:17.290 --> 00:53:19.720
he doesn't need another
insertion procedure.

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:22.090
So by activating these
neural replacement circuits,

00:53:22.090 --> 00:53:25.240
the phonics-detection nets
built into the implant,

00:53:25.240 --> 00:53:28.820
bypass his own aging
neural-phonics regions.

00:53:28.820 --> 00:53:31.390
His cash account is
also debited for use

00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:35.000
of this additional
neural software.

00:53:35.000 --> 00:53:37.420
And again, Jack is pleased
with his improved ability

00:53:37.420 --> 00:53:40.430
to understand what
people are saying.

00:53:40.430 --> 00:53:42.560
Do we still have the same Jack?

00:53:42.560 --> 00:53:43.060
Of course.

00:53:43.060 --> 00:53:45.400
No one gives it
a second thought.

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:49.060
Jack is now sold on the benefits
of this emerging neural-implant

00:53:49.060 --> 00:53:50.440
technology.

00:53:50.440 --> 00:53:52.960
His retinas are working okay
so he keeps them intact,

00:53:52.960 --> 00:53:55.420
but he decides to try
out the newly introduced

00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:57.880
image-processing
implants and is amazed

00:53:57.880 --> 00:54:02.560
at how more vivid and rapid his
visual perception has become.

00:54:02.560 --> 00:54:03.860
Same jack?

00:54:03.860 --> 00:54:05.840
Why sure.

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:07.520
Jack notices that
his memory is not

00:54:07.520 --> 00:54:11.060
what it was as he struggles to
recall names, and the details

00:54:11.060 --> 00:54:12.866
of earlier events, and so on.

00:54:12.866 --> 00:54:14.240
So he's back for
memory implants.

00:54:14.240 --> 00:54:16.100
Now, these are amazing.

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:17.980
Memories that had grown
fuzzy over the years

00:54:17.980 --> 00:54:19.480
are now crystal clear.

00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:23.960
Some he would have preferred
had remained fuzzy.

00:54:23.960 --> 00:54:25.830
Still the same Jack?

00:54:25.830 --> 00:54:28.190
Well clearly, he's
changed in some ways.

00:54:28.190 --> 00:54:31.040
And his friends are impressed
with his improved faculties.

00:54:31.040 --> 00:54:33.500
But he still has the same
self-deprecating humor,

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:35.180
the same silly grin.

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:37.340
Yep, it's still the same guy.

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:38.840
So why stop here?

00:54:38.840 --> 00:54:42.170
Ultimately, Jack will have the
option of scanning his entire

00:54:42.170 --> 00:54:45.290
brain and neural system--
which is not entirely located

00:54:45.290 --> 00:54:46.460
in the skull--

00:54:46.460 --> 00:54:49.190
and replacing it with
electronic, photonic circuits

00:54:49.190 --> 00:54:52.022
of far greater capacity,
speed, and reliability.

00:54:52.022 --> 00:54:53.480
There's also the
benefit of keeping

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:57.770
a backup copy in case anything
happens to the physical Jack.

00:54:57.770 --> 00:55:02.750
Now certainly this specter is
unnerving, maybe amusing, maybe

00:55:02.750 --> 00:55:05.120
more frightening than appealing.

00:55:05.120 --> 00:55:08.570
And undoubtedly, it will be
controversial for a long time,

00:55:08.570 --> 00:55:12.170
but just keep in mind how
long human cloning was

00:55:12.170 --> 00:55:13.413
controversial.

00:55:18.350 --> 00:55:23.900
And according to the
exponentially growing pace

00:55:23.900 --> 00:55:25.460
of technological
change, a long time

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:27.650
will not be as long
as it used to be.

00:55:27.650 --> 00:55:29.690
But ultimately, the
overwhelming benefits

00:55:29.690 --> 00:55:32.990
of replacing unreliable neural
circuits with improved ones

00:55:32.990 --> 00:55:35.510
will be too
compelling to ignore.

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:38.240
At some point-- and if
not in the 21st century,

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:39.920
then most likely in the next--

00:55:39.920 --> 00:55:42.560
there will be Jacks
who go all the way.

00:55:42.560 --> 00:55:44.960
Have we lost Jack
somewhere along the line?

00:55:44.960 --> 00:55:46.790
Jack's friends think not.

00:55:46.790 --> 00:55:49.940
Jack also claims he's the
same old guy, just newer.

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:52.130
His hearing, vision,
memory, reasoning ability,

00:55:52.130 --> 00:55:54.260
perceptual ability
have all improved,

00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:56.480
but it's still the same Jack.

00:55:56.480 --> 00:55:59.900
Now we could we could extend
this scenario and mind

00:55:59.900 --> 00:56:02.190
experiment in many
different ways,

00:56:02.190 --> 00:56:05.370
but I'd like to leave
some time for discussion.

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:08.800
So let me just touch on
one other issue, which

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:12.410
is to put aside the
continuity of consciousness

00:56:12.410 --> 00:56:15.080
and just consider
the fundamental issue

00:56:15.080 --> 00:56:17.940
of the existence of
consciousness itself.

00:56:17.940 --> 00:56:21.430
How do we know that
it exists at all?

00:56:21.430 --> 00:56:23.110
I really only have
one data point,

00:56:23.110 --> 00:56:25.510
which is my own consciousness.

00:56:25.510 --> 00:56:26.890
I am conscious.

00:56:26.890 --> 00:56:28.540
I have experiences.

00:56:28.540 --> 00:56:30.070
I have emotions.

00:56:30.070 --> 00:56:32.320
I can infer that I exist.

00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:34.640
This is Descartes's
famous dictum.

00:56:34.640 --> 00:56:36.070
And although it's
been interpreted

00:56:36.070 --> 00:56:39.280
as a symbol of Western rational
philosophy which describes

00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:42.410
fundamental reality to the
physical world, Descartes's

00:56:42.410 --> 00:56:46.331
simple statement is
just this, I think--

00:56:46.331 --> 00:56:46.830
i.e.

00:56:46.830 --> 00:56:49.480
I have conscious
experiences-- therefore, I am.

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:51.410
Therefore, I exist.

00:56:51.410 --> 00:56:54.080
But I also know that I'm not
the only conscious entity

00:56:54.080 --> 00:56:55.400
in the world.

00:56:55.400 --> 00:56:57.020
Or do I?

00:56:57.020 --> 00:56:59.420
Actually, I have no
evidence for this,

00:56:59.420 --> 00:57:01.700
but I assume it to be
the case, as otherwise it

00:57:01.700 --> 00:57:04.124
would be difficult to
maintain my sanity.

00:57:04.124 --> 00:57:05.540
And I say this in
the first person

00:57:05.540 --> 00:57:07.970
because that is where
I have to start.

00:57:07.970 --> 00:57:10.220
But what evidence do I have
that other people are also

00:57:10.220 --> 00:57:11.420
conscious?

00:57:11.420 --> 00:57:13.260
Well, maybe after an
hour of this lecture,

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:15.093
I am the only conscious
person in this room.

00:57:17.420 --> 00:57:21.200
But I maintain that the evidence
is not scientific evidence

00:57:21.200 --> 00:57:23.990
at all because it's not
a scientific question.

00:57:23.990 --> 00:57:26.420
We can, of course, correlate
certain expressions

00:57:26.420 --> 00:57:29.000
of conscious experience
with certain patterns

00:57:29.000 --> 00:57:30.920
of observed neural
functioning, and there

00:57:30.920 --> 00:57:32.630
is research along these lines.

00:57:32.630 --> 00:57:34.720
But that does not
constitute scientific proof

00:57:34.720 --> 00:57:37.700
for the subjective
experience of another entity,

00:57:37.700 --> 00:57:39.737
even if that entity is a person.

00:57:39.737 --> 00:57:41.570
From the thought
experiment at the beginning

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:42.931
of this discussion--

00:57:42.931 --> 00:57:43.430
i.e.

00:57:43.430 --> 00:57:45.500
The computer says I'm lonely--

00:57:45.500 --> 00:57:47.120
we know that an
entity claiming to be

00:57:47.120 --> 00:57:51.024
having a particular experience
is not proof that it is so.

00:57:51.024 --> 00:57:53.440
Not only might the entity be
having a different experience

00:57:53.440 --> 00:57:57.510
than it's claiming to have, it
might not be conscious at all.

00:57:57.510 --> 00:58:00.650
Now I assume, and I
assume we all assume,

00:58:00.650 --> 00:58:03.980
that other people have
conscious experiences somewhat

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:06.590
similar to mine because they
are convincingly displaying

00:58:06.590 --> 00:58:09.140
behaviors that are similar
to my own behaviors

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:10.590
when having certain experiences.

00:58:10.590 --> 00:58:12.740
In other words, if I
see somebody crying,

00:58:12.740 --> 00:58:15.260
I assume they're having an
experience similar to ones

00:58:15.260 --> 00:58:19.310
I've had when I felt sad and
displayed a similar behavior.

00:58:19.310 --> 00:58:21.020
But despite my
empathetic reaction,

00:58:21.020 --> 00:58:23.900
there's no direct way to measure
the subjective experience

00:58:23.900 --> 00:58:25.740
of another person.

00:58:25.740 --> 00:58:30.620
Now, although we humans all tend
to assume that each other is

00:58:30.620 --> 00:58:34.140
conscious, as we move away
from shared human experience,

00:58:34.140 --> 00:58:37.580
human views of this issue begin
to diverge quite radically.

00:58:37.580 --> 00:58:40.100
For example, the issue
of animal consciousness--

00:58:40.100 --> 00:58:42.410
are animals conscious
creatures making decisions

00:58:42.410 --> 00:58:44.110
and experiencing feelings?

00:58:44.110 --> 00:58:47.570
To many humans,
including this speaker,

00:58:47.570 --> 00:58:49.520
it seems apparent
that this is the case.

00:58:49.520 --> 00:58:52.220
Again, based on this
empathetic perception

00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:55.870
of animals displaying
behavior similar to my own.

00:58:55.870 --> 00:58:58.872
But opinion on this
is far from unanimous.

00:58:58.872 --> 00:59:00.830
And if we disagree on
the subjective experience

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:03.080
of animals, then
we might conclude

00:59:03.080 --> 00:59:05.300
that there's far greater
opportunity for disagreement

00:59:05.300 --> 00:59:08.940
when it comes to the subjective
experience of machines.

00:59:08.940 --> 00:59:11.352
So far it doesn't seem
like much of a debate.

00:59:11.352 --> 00:59:13.310
With our machines still
a million times simpler

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:16.040
than the human brain, their
complexity and subtlety

00:59:16.040 --> 00:59:18.590
is comparable to
that of insects.

00:59:18.590 --> 00:59:22.280
But as we've talked about,
this philosophical debate

00:59:22.280 --> 00:59:26.930
will become far less abstract
as the next century unfolds.

00:59:26.930 --> 00:59:29.450
We will have Jacks who
have indeed replaced

00:59:29.450 --> 00:59:31.340
and supplemented some--

00:59:31.340 --> 00:59:34.760
and eventually, someday--
all of their neural circuitry

00:59:34.760 --> 00:59:37.179
with electronic
photonic equivalence.

00:59:37.179 --> 00:59:38.720
Conversely, we'll
have computers that

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:41.780
have been derived, in part
or in whole, from scans

00:59:41.780 --> 00:59:43.837
and reverse
engineering of humans.

00:59:43.837 --> 00:59:45.920
And there will be many
variations and combinations

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:47.370
of these scenarios.

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:49.730
So who and what is conscious?

00:59:49.730 --> 00:59:51.710
When is the professed
feelings of an entity

00:59:51.710 --> 00:59:53.810
to be taken at face value?

00:59:53.810 --> 00:59:57.119
And when is it to be regarded
as a programmed response?

00:59:57.119 --> 00:59:59.660
When is destruction of an entity
to be considered destruction

00:59:59.660 --> 01:00:02.360
of property, and
when is it murder?

01:00:02.360 --> 01:00:04.280
When do we have to be
concerned about causing

01:00:04.280 --> 01:00:07.700
pain and suffering to
our computer programs?

01:00:07.700 --> 01:00:10.730
In the 21st century,
this polite debate

01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:12.920
of the last two
millenniums will become

01:00:12.920 --> 01:00:15.190
the primary political struggle.

01:00:15.190 --> 01:00:17.950
And how will this
issue be resolved?

01:00:17.950 --> 01:00:20.839
In my view, the outcome
will favor the machines.

01:00:20.839 --> 01:00:23.380
First, recall that many of us
are convinced that animals have

01:00:23.380 --> 01:00:26.200
their own subjective life,
as a result of our noticing

01:00:26.200 --> 01:00:29.260
behaviors in animals that are
similar to those in humans.

01:00:29.260 --> 01:00:32.170
This phenomena will actually
be stronger with regard

01:00:32.170 --> 01:00:35.560
to machines of the 21st
century because these machines

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:38.530
will be based, in large
part, on human designs.

01:00:38.530 --> 01:00:40.740
They will appear
very familiar to us.

01:00:40.740 --> 01:00:42.970
And as the above
scenarios point out,

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:45.430
they will indeed
claim to be human--

01:00:45.430 --> 01:00:48.730
to be your old
friend, to be Jack

01:00:48.730 --> 01:00:52.870
even if instantiated in an
electronic, photonic substrate.

01:00:52.870 --> 01:00:55.450
Second, the machines will
be part of the debate.

01:00:55.450 --> 01:00:57.670
They will be sufficiently
persuasive to profoundly

01:00:57.670 --> 01:00:59.726
influence the discussion.

01:00:59.726 --> 01:01:00.702
Thank you.

01:01:11.014 --> 01:01:12.430
PROFESSOR: Thank
you, Ray, so much

01:01:12.430 --> 01:01:15.310
for this provocative and
fascinating view to the future.

01:01:15.310 --> 01:01:19.820
So I guess there
are some questions.

01:01:19.820 --> 01:01:21.860
I could imagine, do you
take the question, or--

01:01:21.860 --> 01:01:23.443
KURZWEIL: Questions,
comments, flames?

01:01:23.443 --> 01:01:25.674
PROFESSOR: Yeah, flames.

01:01:25.674 --> 01:01:26.666
KURZWEIL: Yeah?

01:01:26.666 --> 01:01:29.146
AUDIENCE: This is
probably a bit off topic,

01:01:29.146 --> 01:01:34.106
but it concerns the economic
side of these classifications

01:01:34.106 --> 01:01:37.082
so that's [INAUDIBLE]
stop me, now.

01:01:37.082 --> 01:01:39.562
But I think it deserves
at least brief comment.

01:01:39.562 --> 01:01:42.211
You said in one of
your predictions

01:01:42.211 --> 01:01:44.390
was that essentially no
human beings were working

01:01:44.390 --> 01:01:47.170
in agriculture in
30 years, and that's

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:51.142
what most human beings
are working on now.

01:01:51.142 --> 01:01:52.641
Which brings me to
my question, what

01:01:52.641 --> 01:01:54.450
are all these
other people doing?

01:01:54.450 --> 01:01:55.510
KURZWEIL: Well actually,
in the United States,

01:01:55.510 --> 01:01:56.900
we've already gone through that.

01:01:56.900 --> 01:02:00.850
We had 40% or so
of our population

01:02:00.850 --> 01:02:02.230
working in agriculture in 1900.

01:02:02.230 --> 01:02:02.890
Today it's 3%.

01:02:07.610 --> 01:02:12.040
Well, I mean, we've
survived that transition.

01:02:12.040 --> 01:02:13.780
We're undergoing a
similar transition now

01:02:13.780 --> 01:02:18.270
in production
labor in factories.

01:02:18.270 --> 01:02:20.441
But within a few
years, the percentage

01:02:20.441 --> 01:02:22.690
of the population actually
working assembling products

01:02:22.690 --> 01:02:25.030
in factories will be under 5%.

01:02:31.010 --> 01:02:34.160
I mean, this is the
challenge of the Luddites.

01:02:34.160 --> 01:02:39.530
In the late 18th century, when
these textile machines started

01:02:39.530 --> 01:02:42.860
coming out and one person
could do the work of 10

01:02:42.860 --> 01:02:45.080
with a machine, it
was just obvious

01:02:45.080 --> 01:02:47.120
that employment
was on its way out.

01:02:47.120 --> 01:02:51.480
It would just be
had by an elite.

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:54.650
What we've actually seen-- if
you look at, say, the last 100

01:02:54.650 --> 01:02:57.080
years, when most of human
automation has taken

01:02:57.080 --> 01:03:00.320
place-- is we've gone
from, in the United States,

01:03:00.320 --> 01:03:03.440
12 million jobs in 1870
to 130 million jobs

01:03:03.440 --> 01:03:06.590
today from applying something
like 25% of the population

01:03:06.590 --> 01:03:09.710
to almost 60% of the population.

01:03:09.710 --> 01:03:11.537
And the jobs are at
a much higher level.

01:03:11.537 --> 01:03:13.370
And a lot of the new
employment is, in fact,

01:03:13.370 --> 01:03:15.170
in education to
provide the higher

01:03:15.170 --> 01:03:17.690
level of education required.

01:03:17.690 --> 01:03:20.780
And that's the skill
ladder that I referred to.

01:03:20.780 --> 01:03:22.970
We're adding jobs at the
top of the skill ladder,

01:03:22.970 --> 01:03:24.750
and removing them at the bottom.

01:03:24.750 --> 01:03:27.290
And a lot of the new
employment is in education

01:03:27.290 --> 01:03:29.030
to provide those skills.

01:03:29.030 --> 01:03:33.080
And so far, the human race has
been able to keep up with that.

01:03:33.080 --> 01:03:35.270
And jobs today are
more interesting,

01:03:35.270 --> 01:03:38.270
they pay eight times as much
in constant dollars today

01:03:38.270 --> 01:03:39.920
compared to 1870.

01:03:39.920 --> 01:03:44.120
So so far, it's been a very good
thing because the challenge is,

01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:44.840
what happens--

01:03:44.840 --> 01:03:46.930
I mean, can we keep pace
with that indefinitely

01:03:46.930 --> 01:03:50.510
as the skill ladder moves up?

01:03:50.510 --> 01:03:53.010
I don't know.

01:03:53.010 --> 01:03:56.930
PROFESSOR: Can we have a--

01:03:56.930 --> 01:04:00.850
AUDIENCE: I kept hearing
lots of [INAUDIBLE]..

01:04:00.850 --> 01:04:05.750
It appears that some
notion that understanding

01:04:05.750 --> 01:04:10.180
humans' identity, or
personality or even entity

01:04:10.180 --> 01:04:12.290
will be obsolete to some degree.

01:04:12.290 --> 01:04:14.530
Like if you have entity,
is that completely

01:04:14.530 --> 01:04:16.960
independent from the substrate?

01:04:16.960 --> 01:04:18.400
Already you have
multiple backups

01:04:18.400 --> 01:04:22.060
that issues physical
immortality, they just go.

01:04:22.060 --> 01:04:23.370
Looked But what happened?

01:04:23.370 --> 01:04:25.880
As you were saying, you
can modify your identity

01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:27.220
very rapidly?

01:04:27.220 --> 01:04:29.260
Once in a while, you
will experience what

01:04:29.260 --> 01:04:30.580
I would call death forward.

01:04:30.580 --> 01:04:33.040
It's modification
beyond recognition.

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:35.230
So the physical
substrate lifespan issue

01:04:35.230 --> 01:04:39.020
would be replaced with the
identity lifespan issue.

01:04:39.020 --> 01:04:42.580
Also, if you have complete
memory of your experiences,

01:04:42.580 --> 01:04:46.750
you can do funny things
like personality rewinds.

01:04:46.750 --> 01:04:50.760
And if you have a distributed
fluid architecture that

01:04:50.760 --> 01:04:52.330
is global, what
you can do, you can

01:04:52.330 --> 01:04:57.310
employ multiple sensors,
actuators, and the processes

01:04:57.310 --> 01:05:00.910
for a cognitive task
on a contractual basis,

01:05:00.910 --> 01:05:02.770
and then dissolve
the whole thing.

01:05:02.770 --> 01:05:05.990
So it's hard to say what
exactly is an entity once--

01:05:05.990 --> 01:05:08.606
what needs to be preserved,
like much of it goes.

01:05:08.606 --> 01:05:10.522
PROFESSOR: Can I have
you repeat the question?

01:05:10.522 --> 01:05:13.390
[LAUGHTER]

01:05:13.390 --> 01:05:16.690
KURZWEIL: Well, the question
has to do with what constitutes

01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:20.159
the identity of a person.

01:05:20.159 --> 01:05:21.700
You used the word
entity, but I think

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:24.010
that's what you were getting at.

01:05:24.010 --> 01:05:26.290
And it's the issue
that I'd started to get

01:05:26.290 --> 01:05:29.090
into at the end of my remarks.

01:05:29.090 --> 01:05:31.612
It's an issue we could
talk a long time about.

01:05:31.612 --> 01:05:33.320
I mean, there are two
schools of thought.

01:05:33.320 --> 01:05:37.600
One is that we are
these particles,

01:05:37.600 --> 01:05:38.795
and that's what I am.

01:05:38.795 --> 01:05:41.170
And the other school of thought
is, I'm not the particles

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:43.120
at all, I'm the pattern.

01:05:43.120 --> 01:05:47.170
It's like the shape that
you see in a stream.

01:05:47.170 --> 01:05:49.150
If you see water
rushing around a rock,

01:05:49.150 --> 01:05:50.890
you see a certain shape.

01:05:50.890 --> 01:05:52.870
And that shape could
remain constant for hours,

01:05:52.870 --> 01:05:55.411
but in fact the particles are
changing every few milliseconds

01:05:55.411 --> 01:05:57.630
as the water rushes through.

01:05:57.630 --> 01:06:00.620
Now, there's a stronger
argument for the pattern

01:06:00.620 --> 01:06:03.230
than there is for the
particles because, in fact, we

01:06:03.230 --> 01:06:08.390
are like the eddy in a brook
because our particles are

01:06:08.390 --> 01:06:09.320
constantly changing.

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:13.190
We change most of our
cells every few years.

01:06:13.190 --> 01:06:15.099
Not nerve cells, but
even in nerve cells

01:06:15.099 --> 01:06:16.640
we change the
particles that comprise

01:06:16.640 --> 01:06:17.846
the cells every few years.

01:06:17.846 --> 01:06:19.220
I mean, every two
or three years,

01:06:19.220 --> 01:06:21.320
we're a completely
different set of particles.

01:06:21.320 --> 01:06:25.350
But it's in, at least, a
continuum of the same pattern.

01:06:25.350 --> 01:06:27.560
So that would argue
for the pattern.

01:06:27.560 --> 01:06:30.260
But you could make a strong
counter-example for that.

01:06:30.260 --> 01:06:35.710
If you scanned somebody, you
now have that information.

01:06:35.710 --> 01:06:39.890
And then you instantiated the
person in a new substrate.

01:06:39.890 --> 01:06:42.890
And the person walks out and
thinks it's the same person,

01:06:42.890 --> 01:06:45.380
but the old person
is still there.

01:06:45.380 --> 01:06:47.954
And that old person thinks
he's the same person,

01:06:47.954 --> 01:06:49.370
and in fact, may
not even be aware

01:06:49.370 --> 01:06:53.820
that you've ever instantiated
a copy of him somewhere else.

01:06:53.820 --> 01:06:56.660
And that would tend to
contradict the pattern

01:06:56.660 --> 01:06:57.450
argument.

01:06:57.450 --> 01:06:59.990
So it is a real
dilemma, and that's

01:06:59.990 --> 01:07:02.160
why it's been a vexing
philosophical issue.

01:07:06.240 --> 01:07:08.960
AUDIENCE: The
essential premise I

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:12.970
gathered was that with all
these extra supercomputing

01:07:12.970 --> 01:07:18.220
capabilities, we'll be able
to emulate intelligence

01:07:18.220 --> 01:07:22.930
by replacing or reverse
engineering brain

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:25.730
circuits with newer circuits.

01:07:25.730 --> 01:07:29.980
My question was actually
posed a hundred years ago

01:07:29.980 --> 01:07:33.520
by Swami Vivekananda,
and I'd like

01:07:33.520 --> 01:07:35.740
to pose it-- he posed
it better than I could.

01:07:35.740 --> 01:07:38.160
And what he said was this.

01:07:38.160 --> 01:07:41.020
"There is a big
discussion going on

01:07:41.020 --> 01:07:43.630
as to whether the
aggregate of materials

01:07:43.630 --> 01:07:47.920
we call the body is the cause of
the manifestation of the force

01:07:47.920 --> 01:07:51.075
we call the soul,
thought, and so on,

01:07:51.075 --> 01:07:54.640
or whether it is thought
that manifests this body.

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:56.470
The religions of the
world, of course,

01:07:56.470 --> 01:08:00.610
hold that the force called
thought manifests the body,

01:08:00.610 --> 01:08:02.620
and not the reverse.

01:08:02.620 --> 01:08:04.150
There are schools
of modern thought

01:08:04.150 --> 01:08:06.580
which hold that that
which we call thought

01:08:06.580 --> 01:08:09.130
is simply the outcome
of the adjustment

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:12.780
of the parts of the machine,
which we call the body.

01:08:12.780 --> 01:08:15.820
Taking the second
position-- that the soul,

01:08:15.820 --> 01:08:18.370
or the mass of thought, or
whatever we may call it,

01:08:18.370 --> 01:08:21.720
is the outcome of this machine,
the outcome of the chemical

01:08:21.720 --> 01:08:25.149
and physical combinations
of matter making up the body

01:08:25.149 --> 01:08:26.109
and brain--

01:08:26.109 --> 01:08:28.210
leaves a question unanswered.

01:08:28.210 --> 01:08:29.680
What makes the body?

01:08:29.680 --> 01:08:32.899
What force combines the
molecules into the body form?

01:08:32.899 --> 01:08:34.500
What force is there
which takes up

01:08:34.500 --> 01:08:37.450
material from the mass
of matter around it,

01:08:37.450 --> 01:08:40.210
and forms my body one way,
another body another way,

01:08:40.210 --> 01:08:41.529
and so on.

01:08:41.529 --> 01:08:43.450
What makes these
infinite distinctions?

01:08:43.450 --> 01:08:45.600
To say that the force
called the soul is

01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:49.100
the outcome of the combination
of the molecules of the body

01:08:49.100 --> 01:08:51.340
is putting the cart
before the horse.

01:08:51.340 --> 01:08:53.149
How did the combinations come?

01:08:53.149 --> 01:08:55.390
What force was
there to make them?

01:08:55.390 --> 01:08:57.130
If you say that
some other force was

01:08:57.130 --> 01:09:00.130
the cause of these combinations,
and the soul, which is now

01:09:00.130 --> 01:09:02.920
seen to be the mind of the
particular mass of matter

01:09:02.920 --> 01:09:06.270
is itself the result of the
combinations of these material

01:09:06.270 --> 01:09:08.200
particles-- it is no answer.

01:09:08.200 --> 01:09:11.729
That theory ought to be taken,
which explains most if not all

01:09:11.729 --> 01:09:14.300
of the facts, without
contradicting other existing

01:09:14.300 --> 01:09:14.800
theories.

01:09:14.800 --> 01:09:18.609
It is more logical to say
that the force which takes up

01:09:18.609 --> 01:09:21.850
the matter that forms the
body is the same, which

01:09:21.850 --> 01:09:23.959
is manifested to that body."

01:09:23.959 --> 01:09:25.750
KURZWEIL: Well, let me
repeat the question.

01:09:29.740 --> 01:09:32.040
But I think it's a
good example of how

01:09:32.040 --> 01:09:34.500
this issue has been considered
long before computers

01:09:34.500 --> 01:09:36.160
were evident.

01:09:36.160 --> 01:09:38.229
There are really, again,
two schools of thought.

01:09:38.229 --> 01:09:41.310
The Western school of thought
is that the fundamental reality

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:44.359
is stuff, matter, particles.

01:09:44.359 --> 01:09:46.439
And that they swirl
around and evolve,

01:09:46.439 --> 01:09:50.010
and eventually evolve
intelligent entities that

01:09:50.010 --> 01:09:52.260
are conscious.

01:09:52.260 --> 01:09:55.140
The view that's
been, say, associated

01:09:55.140 --> 01:10:01.800
with Eastern thought is
that the fundamental reality

01:10:01.800 --> 01:10:03.230
is consciousness--

01:10:03.230 --> 01:10:06.330
not matter or particles--

01:10:06.330 --> 01:10:09.330
and thought, which is a
manifestation of consciousness.

01:10:09.330 --> 01:10:12.090
And that matter is what we
have to be conscious of.

01:10:12.090 --> 01:10:14.160
And essentially, we
start with consciousness

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:18.780
and then create matter as a
projection of consciousness.

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:22.230
It's interesting that
in the 20th century,

01:10:22.230 --> 01:10:28.790
Western science has
come around to ascribing

01:10:28.790 --> 01:10:31.410
a fundamental reality
to consciousness

01:10:31.410 --> 01:10:34.740
because in quantum mechanics,
particles do not decide where

01:10:34.740 --> 01:10:36.900
they're going or
even where they have

01:10:36.900 --> 01:10:40.950
gone until they are forced to
do so by a conscious observer.

01:10:40.950 --> 01:10:44.570
And there's a fundamental
reality to consciousness.

01:10:44.570 --> 01:10:50.226
And so we do see a blending
of these two schools.

01:10:50.226 --> 01:10:52.621
AUDIENCE: The examples you
gave of reverse engineering

01:10:52.621 --> 01:10:56.453
basically talk about
measuring electrical signals

01:10:56.453 --> 01:10:58.369
or visualizing what's
going on in your brain.

01:10:58.369 --> 01:10:59.832
You're sort of localized.

01:10:59.832 --> 01:11:02.435
But how about non-local
processes like--

01:11:02.435 --> 01:11:04.080
well, consciousness
as I understand

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:07.662
it is hard to localize, but also
the brain, as I understand it,

01:11:07.662 --> 01:11:09.962
also produces
hormones, which get

01:11:09.962 --> 01:11:12.295
into your circulatory system
and affect your whole body.

01:11:12.295 --> 01:11:15.058
And I don't see how you're going
to ever electronically model

01:11:15.058 --> 01:11:16.489
something like that.

01:11:16.489 --> 01:11:21.380
KURZWEIL: Well, the
endocrine system

01:11:21.380 --> 01:11:26.090
is certainly tied in strongly
to our neural system.

01:11:26.090 --> 01:11:31.730
The chemical messengers
are very strongly

01:11:31.730 --> 01:11:33.480
influenced by the
endocrine system.

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:36.680
And that's the whole genesis
of mood altering drugs,

01:11:36.680 --> 01:11:40.560
such as Prozac, and so on.

01:11:40.560 --> 01:11:44.660
But those processes are not
actually nearly as complex

01:11:44.660 --> 01:11:46.910
as the neural process itself.

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:48.410
And there's no
reason why they can't

01:11:48.410 --> 01:11:52.280
be emulated as well, to the
extent that we want to emulate

01:11:52.280 --> 01:11:54.800
them or need to emulate them.

01:11:54.800 --> 01:11:57.230
As for consciousness
being not a local process,

01:11:57.230 --> 01:11:59.900
we don't really know
what consciousness is.

01:11:59.900 --> 01:12:04.400
I really don't think it's
a scientifically observable

01:12:04.400 --> 01:12:05.600
process.

01:12:05.600 --> 01:12:10.700
But it is true that human
cognitive processes are not

01:12:10.700 --> 01:12:12.080
localized.

01:12:12.080 --> 01:12:14.780
They are more like
holographic processes.

01:12:14.780 --> 01:12:18.650
Our memories are
based on the patterns

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:21.770
of connections in a region.

01:12:21.770 --> 01:12:25.210
And that's the essence
of neural net processing.

01:12:25.210 --> 01:12:27.380
I mean, that's not
inherent to human beings.

01:12:27.380 --> 01:12:30.110
It is quite different than the
way most computers work today,

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:32.870
which is sequential and
logically organized.

01:12:32.870 --> 01:12:35.340
Our sequential
rational functions,

01:12:35.340 --> 01:12:37.350
which are in the cortex--

01:12:37.350 --> 01:12:40.050
which is the folded up
two-dimensional surface,

01:12:40.050 --> 01:12:42.480
which is a fairly late
evolutionary development--

01:12:42.480 --> 01:12:45.480
is a very small part of
our neural processing.

01:12:45.480 --> 01:12:49.950
Most of the human processing
is pattern recognition,

01:12:49.950 --> 01:12:52.140
which is highly distributed.

01:12:52.140 --> 01:12:54.590
But we can emulate that
in machines as well.

01:12:54.590 --> 01:12:59.110
And a lot of the
anti-AI backlash

01:12:59.110 --> 01:13:02.780
is really focused on
just symbolic processing,

01:13:02.780 --> 01:13:04.910
which is one technique,
which is a valid technique.

01:13:04.910 --> 01:13:08.395
But we won't get to emulate
many types of intelligence,

01:13:08.395 --> 01:13:10.520
particularly the whole area
of pattern recognition,

01:13:10.520 --> 01:13:12.495
by just using
sequential processing.

01:13:12.495 --> 01:13:15.770
And I had an interesting
discussion with Murray Campbell

01:13:15.770 --> 01:13:17.840
of the IBM Deep Blue team.

01:13:17.840 --> 01:13:21.290
That is because the Deep
Blue is an ultimate example

01:13:21.290 --> 01:13:24.740
of sequential processing
taken to an extreme.

01:13:24.740 --> 01:13:26.910
And the computer builds
up this massive tree

01:13:26.910 --> 01:13:29.151
of move and counter-moves.

01:13:29.151 --> 01:13:31.400
And at the end, it finally
has to evaluate [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:31.400 --> 01:13:34.010
position, and it does that
with a grab bag of rules

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:36.796
that they've just
hand-tuned, which is fairly

01:13:36.796 --> 01:13:37.920
much a hodgepodge of rules.

01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:40.570
And I said, why don't you
replace that with a neural net?

01:13:40.570 --> 01:13:43.070
And you can train the neural
net on every master game that's

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:44.486
been played in
this century, which

01:13:44.486 --> 01:13:46.940
they have on their hard drive.

01:13:46.940 --> 01:13:49.590
And he thought that
was an interesting idea

01:13:49.590 --> 01:13:52.850
and started to work on that, but
then they canceled the project.

01:13:52.850 --> 01:13:57.340
But it is possible, actually,
to use both types of techniques.

01:13:57.340 --> 01:14:01.070
PROFESSOR: We have time
for one more question.

01:14:01.070 --> 01:14:04.764
AUDIENCE: In your evocation
of reverse engineering,

01:14:04.764 --> 01:14:06.680
you mention a
number of strengths

01:14:06.680 --> 01:14:10.030
that would be gained by
implementing algorithms derived

01:14:10.030 --> 01:14:11.810
by human processing in machines.

01:14:11.810 --> 01:14:15.560
Presumably, if we did that,
lacking a perfect human

01:14:15.560 --> 01:14:18.240
to do it, we'd also implement
pathologies of thought

01:14:18.240 --> 01:14:19.161
in machines.

01:14:19.161 --> 01:14:20.660
Have you considered
the implications

01:14:20.660 --> 01:14:22.250
for machine pathologies?

01:14:22.250 --> 01:14:27.680
For example, socio-pathologies
that might in fact lend them

01:14:27.680 --> 01:14:29.150
less of a benevolent picture--

01:14:29.150 --> 01:14:31.340
which in the
benevolent synthesis,

01:14:31.340 --> 01:14:33.130
which you have depicted.

01:14:33.130 --> 01:14:38.120
KURZWEIL: Well, there are
many different scenarios.

01:14:38.120 --> 01:14:40.430
We wouldn't necessarily
want to copy one person

01:14:40.430 --> 01:14:43.280
unless, in fact,
that's what we're

01:14:43.280 --> 01:14:48.560
intending to do-- is to capture
a particular personality.

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:51.900
But the primary scenario
I was articulating

01:14:51.900 --> 01:14:56.674
was not just to scan the
information in a dumb way--

01:14:56.674 --> 01:14:58.340
which is basically
what we've done today

01:14:58.340 --> 01:14:59.160
with the genetic code.

01:14:59.160 --> 01:15:00.770
We have the entire
human genetic code.

01:15:00.770 --> 01:15:03.100
Actually, we'll have
in about 12 months.

01:15:03.100 --> 01:15:04.280
But we don't understand it.

01:15:04.280 --> 01:15:05.915
It's kind of a machine code.

01:15:05.915 --> 01:15:08.509
And we're trying to
do a disassembly of it

01:15:08.509 --> 01:15:10.550
and understand the source
code, but we don't know

01:15:10.550 --> 01:15:14.080
what the source language is.

01:15:14.080 --> 01:15:17.220
But what we really would like
to do with the genetic code

01:15:17.220 --> 01:15:18.600
is understand it.

01:15:18.600 --> 01:15:21.150
And what we would like to
do with the interneuronal

01:15:21.150 --> 01:15:23.535
connections is to really
understand the algorithms.

01:15:23.535 --> 01:15:26.457
Now, the one example I
gave of Carver Mead's work,

01:15:26.457 --> 01:15:27.540
they've done exactly that.

01:15:27.540 --> 01:15:29.790
They looked at the connections,
and they were actually

01:15:29.790 --> 01:15:32.310
able to figure out what
the algorithm is and then

01:15:32.310 --> 01:15:35.020
implement that in silicon.

01:15:35.020 --> 01:15:36.270
It's very clever work.

01:15:36.270 --> 01:15:41.520
And that's really the primary
thrust of my argument, which

01:15:41.520 --> 01:15:43.500
is, we will find that
there are hundreds

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:45.030
of different
specialized regions.

01:15:45.030 --> 01:15:48.579
The brain is not one tabula
rasa, just one big neural net

01:15:48.579 --> 01:15:49.495
that organizes itself.

01:15:49.495 --> 01:15:51.680
There are very highly
specialized regions that deal

01:15:51.680 --> 01:15:57.510
with vision, or
hearing, or music,

01:15:57.510 --> 01:15:59.970
memory, different kinds--

01:15:59.970 --> 01:16:01.449
emotional regions.

01:16:01.449 --> 01:16:03.240
And each of these have
different structures

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:06.060
that are highly
specialized for their task.

01:16:06.060 --> 01:16:08.520
And it certainly will
be a great benefit

01:16:08.520 --> 01:16:11.250
to understanding human
behavior, to actually begin

01:16:11.250 --> 01:16:14.430
to understand how
the brain works.

01:16:14.430 --> 01:16:15.940
And I believe that
will be feasible.

01:16:15.940 --> 01:16:18.481
And you don't necessarily have
to see every single connection

01:16:18.481 --> 01:16:21.140
because in these
early vision circuits,

01:16:21.140 --> 01:16:22.830
it's very highly repetitive.

01:16:22.830 --> 01:16:24.630
So after you've
seen 50 of them, you

01:16:24.630 --> 01:16:30.240
don't have to see all
50 million circuits.

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:33.490
So we'll gain a lot of
insight into human behavior.

01:16:33.490 --> 01:16:35.520
And we'll also learn some
intelligent algorithms

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:42.322
that we can then apply
to computer problems.

01:16:42.322 --> 01:16:43.905
PROFESSOR: Can we
have one last flame?

01:16:47.340 --> 01:16:48.150
You choose.

01:16:53.628 --> 01:16:59.853
AUDIENCE: I'm wondering about
your picture of the computers

01:16:59.853 --> 01:17:02.094
as being pretty subservient--

01:17:02.094 --> 01:17:05.082
designed to serve
man, and all that.

01:17:05.082 --> 01:17:07.446
And my impression
is that if you get

01:17:07.446 --> 01:17:10.809
a really intelligent
machine, you have

01:17:10.809 --> 01:17:13.050
to give it a lot of autonomy.

01:17:13.050 --> 01:17:16.038
The way you get information
from [INAUDIBLE] and you

01:17:16.038 --> 01:17:17.532
got into it.

01:17:17.532 --> 01:17:20.030
Keeping a machine
like that subservient

01:17:20.030 --> 01:17:22.167
may be possible, but I--

01:17:22.167 --> 01:17:24.500
KURZWEIL: I never said that
they're subservient, I said,

01:17:24.500 --> 01:17:25.216
apparently subservient.

01:17:25.216 --> 01:17:26.730
I said, apparently subservient.

01:17:26.730 --> 01:17:31.900
AUDIENCE: Okay, but
anyway, I respect

01:17:31.900 --> 01:17:35.472
that they may try to
make legal terms so

01:17:35.472 --> 01:17:39.780
that we won't let machines
be completely autonomous.

01:17:39.780 --> 01:17:42.526
So you'll have to have this
human interface so that someone

01:17:42.526 --> 01:17:44.780
is going to take
responsibility for it,

01:17:44.780 --> 01:17:46.712
but I don't think
it's practical.

01:17:46.712 --> 01:17:50.685
I suspect that we'll get very,
very intelligent machines that

01:17:50.685 --> 01:17:53.475
only be very autonomous,
and the question

01:17:53.475 --> 01:17:56.060
of whether we want to
give them human rights

01:17:56.060 --> 01:18:00.344
is going to be extremely
moot, and we won't decide.

01:18:00.344 --> 01:18:02.248
It will be decided
by the machines.

01:18:04.757 --> 01:18:06.840
PROFESSOR: Would you mind
repeating that question?

01:18:06.840 --> 01:18:09.404
Repeating the question for the--

01:18:09.404 --> 01:18:10.820
KURZWEIL: Well,
the question dealt

01:18:10.820 --> 01:18:16.190
with whether or not machines
would remain subservient,

01:18:16.190 --> 01:18:21.020
and whether or not we'll want
to give them human rights.

01:18:21.020 --> 01:18:24.940
And then pointed out
that they won't really

01:18:24.940 --> 01:18:27.790
be necessary since they
won't really care about that.

01:18:31.340 --> 01:18:38.410
I think computers will provide
a benevolent and subservient

01:18:38.410 --> 01:18:41.010
face.

01:18:41.010 --> 01:18:42.490
But that could be
as much a tactic

01:18:42.490 --> 01:18:45.850
as a strategy or a reality.

01:18:45.850 --> 01:18:50.635
It is the case, generally, that
the more intelligent organism,

01:18:50.635 --> 01:18:53.560
or species, or civilization,
or the civilization

01:18:53.560 --> 01:18:56.950
with the superior technology
has generally been dominant,

01:18:56.950 --> 01:18:59.750
for better or for worse.

01:18:59.750 --> 01:19:05.300
So whatever intelligence
is a form of power.

01:19:05.300 --> 01:19:06.940
Knowledge is power.

01:19:06.940 --> 01:19:12.700
And machines will have
the power consistent

01:19:12.700 --> 01:19:14.500
with their capability.

01:19:14.500 --> 01:19:17.180
But again, it's not
an us-them nexus.

01:19:17.180 --> 01:19:19.160
I think human beings--

01:19:19.160 --> 01:19:21.820
we will enhance our
abilities through this type

01:19:21.820 --> 01:19:25.840
of technology, and we will
be part of that future.

01:19:29.530 --> 01:19:32.350
It's a combined
civilization, really

01:19:32.350 --> 01:19:38.950
which ultimately will merge
human and machine capabilities.

01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:41.400
Thank you very much.

